window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-15"]=[{"id":"q2-247","q":"2:247","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:247 · Kingship is not measured by wealth or lineage","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 274–275","cairo":"I:274–275","source_page_first":274,"source_page_last":275,"ar":"وَقالَ لَهُمْ نَبِيُّهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ قَدْ بَعَثَ لَكُمْ طالُوتَ مَلِكاً قالُوا أَنَّى يَكُونُ لَهُ الْمُلْكُ عَلَيْنا وَنَحْنُ أَحَقُّ بِالْمُلْكِ مِنْهُ وَلَمْ يُؤْتَ سَعَةً مِنَ الْمالِ قالَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ اصْطَفاهُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَزادَهُ بَسْطَةً فِي الْعِلْمِ وَالْجِسْمِ وَاللَّهُ يُؤْتِي مُلْكَهُ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (247)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَقالَ لَهُمْ نَبِيُّهُمْ شمويل: إِنَّ اللَّهَ قَدْ بَعَثَ لَكُمْ ملكاً، أي: عيَّنه لكم لتقاتلوا معه، وهو طالُوتَ وهو عَلَمٌ عِبْراني كدَاوُد، قالُوا تعنتاً وتشغيباً: أَنَّى يَكُونُ لَهُ الْمُلْكُ عَلَيْنا أي من أين يستأهل التملك علينا وليس من دار الملك؟ لأن المملكة كانت في أولاد يهوذا، وطالوت من أولاد بنيامين، والنبوة كانت في أولاد لاوى. وقالوا: نَحْنُ أَحَقُّ بِالْمُلْكِ مِنْهُ وراثة ومُكْنة، لأن دار المملكة فينا. وأيضاً هو فقير لَمْ يُؤْتَ سَعَةً مِنَ الْمالِ يتقوى به على حرب عدوه، وكان طالوت فقيراً راعياً أو سَقَّاءً أو دباغاً. قالَ لهم نبيهم- عليه السلام-: إِنَّ اللَّهَ اصْطَفاهُ عَلَيْكُمْ رغم أنفكم. قال وهب بن منبه: أوحى الله إلى نبيهم: إذا دخل عليك رجل فَنَش الدُهنَ(1) الذي في القرن(2) فهو ملكهم، فلما دخل طالوت نَشَّ الدهن.\nوقال السدي: أرسل الله إليه عَصا، وقال له: إذا دخل عليك رجل على طول هذه العصا فهو ملكهم، فكان ذلك طالوت فتبيَّن أن الله تعالى اصطفاه للملك، وَزادَهُ بَسْطَةً فِي الْعِلْمِ فكان أعلم بني إسرائيل بالتوراة وقيل:\nبالحروب وعلم السياسة. وزاده أيضاً بسطة في الْجِسْمِ، فكان أطولُ بني إسرائيل يبلغ إلى مَنْكبِهِ. وذلك ليكون أعظم خطَراً في القلوب، وأقوى على مقاومة العدو ومكابدة الحروب، (والله يؤتي ملكه من يشاء) لأنه ملك الملوك\nيضع مُلكه حيث شاء، (والله واسع) فيوسع على الفقير ويغنيه بلا سبب، (عليم) بمن يليق بالملك بسبب وبلا سبب.\nثم ذكر آية أخرى تدل على ملكه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقالَ لَهُمْ نَبِيُّهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ قَدْ بَعَثَ لَكُمْ طالُوتَ مَلِكاً قالُوا أَنَّى يَكُونُ لَهُ الْمُلْكُ عَلَيْنا وَنَحْنُ أَحَقُّ بِالْمُلْكِ مِنْهُ وَلَمْ يُؤْتَ سَعَةً مِنَ الْمالِ قالَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ اصْطَفاهُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَزادَهُ بَسْطَةً فِي الْعِلْمِ وَالْجِسْمِ وَاللَّهُ يُؤْتِي مُلْكَهُ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (247)","quran_en":"247. Their prophet said to them, “God has appointed Ṭālūt as king for you.” They said, “How can he have kingship over us when we have a stronger right to kingship than he, and he has not been given abundant wealth?” He said, “God has chosen him over you and increased him in knowledge and bodily stature. God grants His kingship to whom He wills. God is All-Encompassing, Knowing.”","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"God’s choice and the qualifications of the king","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: their prophet Shumwīl told them: “God has appointed a king for you”—designated him so that you may fight with him—namely Ṭālūt, a Hebrew proper name like Dāwūd.<\/p><p>In obstinacy and contentiousness, they said: “How can he have kingship over us?” How is he entitled to rule us when he is not of the royal house? Kingship belonged to the descendants of Judah, whereas Ṭālūt descended from Benjamin; prophecy belonged to the descendants of Levi. They said: “We have a stronger right to kingship than he,” by inheritance and power, for the royal house is among us. Moreover, he is poor and has not been given wealth with which to strengthen himself against his enemy. Ṭālūt was a poor shepherd, water-carrier or tanner.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-200\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B189]<\/a><\/p><p>Their prophet said: “God has chosen him over you,” despite your displeasure. Wahb ibn Munabbih said: God revealed to their prophet, “When a man enters your presence and the oil in the horn begins to seethe, he is their king.” When Ṭālūt entered, the oil seethed. Al-Suddī said: God sent him a staff and said, “When a man as tall as this staff enters, he is their king.” This proved to be Ṭālūt, and it became clear that God Most High had chosen him for kingship.<\/p><p>“He increased him in knowledge”: he was the most knowledgeable Israelite in the Torah; or, according to another account, in warfare and the science of government.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-201\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B190]<\/a> He also increased his bodily stature: the tallest Israelite reached only to his shoulder. This made his standing greater in people’s hearts and strengthened him to resist the enemy and endure warfare. “God grants His kingship to whom He wills,” for He is the King of kings,<\/p><p>placing His kingship wherever He wills. God is All-Encompassing: He enriches and enlarges the poor man’s means without an intermediary cause. He knows who is fit for kingship, with or without such a cause.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: their prophet Shumwīl told them: “God has appointed a king for you”—designated him so that you may fight with him—namely Ṭālūt, a Hebrew proper name like Dāwūd. In obstinacy and contentiousness, they said: “How can he have kingship over us?” How is he entitled to rule us when he is not of the royal house? Kingship belonged to the descendants of Judah, whereas Ṭālūt descended from Benjamin; prophecy belonged to the descendants of Levi. They said: “We have a stronger right to kingship than he,” by inheritance and power, for the royal house is among us. Moreover, he is poor and has not been given wealth with which to strengthen himself against his enemy. Ṭālūt was a poor shepherd, water-carrier or tanner. [B189] Their prophet said: “God has chosen him over you,” despite your displeasure. Wahb ibn Munabbih said: God revealed to their prophet, “When a man enters your presence and the oil in the horn begins to seethe, he is their king.” When Ṭālūt entered, the oil seethed. Al-Suddī said: God sent him a staff and said, “When a man as tall as this staff enters, he is their king.” This proved to be Ṭālūt, and it became clear that God Most High had chosen him for kingship. “He increased him in knowledge”: he was the most knowledgeable Israelite in the Torah; or, according to another account, in warfare and the science of government. [B190] He also increased his bodily stature: the tallest Israelite reached only to his shoulder. This made his standing greater in people’s hearts and strengthened him to resist the enemy and endure warfare. “God grants His kingship to whom He wills,” for He is the King of kings, placing His kingship wherever He wills. God is All-Encompassing: He enriches and enlarges the poor man’s means without an intermediary cause. He knows who is fit for kingship, with or without such a cause."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentions another sign indicating his kingship.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentions another sign indicating his kingship."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C274.1","page":274,"ar":"نش الماء ينش نشا ونشيشا ونشش: إذا صوت عند الغليان.","en":"Of water, nashsha (yanishshu; verbal nouns nashshan and nashīshan), or nashshasha: it made a sound when boiling.","anchor":"فَنَش الدُهنَ"},{"id":"C274.2","page":274,"ar":"القرن: هو قرن الثور وغيره. والمراد به هنا: الآنية التي يكون فيها الدهن، وكانوا يتخذونها من قرون البقر وغيرها.","en":"Qarn is the horn of a bull or another animal. Here it means the vessel containing the oil; they used to make such vessels from the horns of cattle and other animals.","anchor":"القرن"}],"english_characters":1926,"arabic_characters":1665},{"id":"q2-248","q":"2:248","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:248 · The Ark, tranquillity and the signs of spiritual election","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 275–276","cairo":"I:275–276","source_page_first":275,"source_page_last":276,"ar":"وَقالَ لَهُمْ نَبِيُّهُمْ إِنَّ آيَةَ مُلْكِهِ أَنْ يَأْتِيَكُمُ التَّابُوتُ فِيهِ سَكِينَةٌ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ وَبَقِيَّةٌ مِمَّا تَرَكَ آلُ مُوسى وَآلُ هارُونَ تَحْمِلُهُ الْمَلائِكَةُ إِنَّ فِي ذلِكَ لَآيَةً لَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (248)\nقلت: قال الجوهري: أصل التابوت: تأبُوة، مثل تَرْقُوة وهي فَعْلُوةٌ، فلما سُكِّنتْ الواو، انقلبت هاء التأنيث تاءٌ، فلغة قريش بالتاء، ولغة الأنصار بالهاء.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَقالَ لَهُمْ نَبِيُّهُمْ لَمَّا طلبوا منه الحجة على اصطفاء طالوت للملك: إِنَّ آيَةَ مُلْكِهِ أَنْ يَأْتِيَكُمُ التَّابُوتُ وهو صندوق من خشب الشمشار مُمَوَّه بالذهب، طوله ثلاثة أذرع في سعة ذراعين فِيهِ سَكِينَةٌ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ أي: فيه ما تسكن إليه قلوبكم وتثبت عند الحرب. وكانوا يُقدمونه أمامهم في الحروب فلا يفرون، ويُنصرون على عدوهم، وقيل: كان فيه صور الأنبياء من آدم عليه السلام إلى محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم. وقيل: كان فيه طَسْت من ذهب غُسلت به قلوب الأنبياء- عليهم السلام- وهى السكينة- وفيه بَقِيَّةٌ مِمَّا تَرَكَ آلُ مُوسى وهي رُضاض الألواح(1)، وعصا موسى، وثيابه، وعمامة هارون والآل: مقحم فيهما.\nتَحْمِلُهُ الْمَلائِكَةُ قال وهب: لما صار التابوت عند القوم الذين غلبوا بني إسرائيل- فوضعوه في كنيسة لهم فيها أصنام، فكانت الأصنام تُصبح مُنكسرة، فحملوه إلى قرية قوم، فأصاب أولئك القومَ أوجاعٌ، فقالوا: ما هذا إلا لهذا التابوت، فلنتركه إلى بني إسرائيل، فأخذوا عَجَلَةً فجعلوا التابوت عليها وربطوها ببقرتين، وأرسلوهما نحو بلاد بني إسرائيل، فبعث الله ملائكة تسوق البقرتين حتى دخلتا على بني إسرائيل، وهم في أمر طالوت فأيقنوا بالنصر.\nوقيل غير ذلك.\nوقوله تعالى: إِنَّ فِي ذلِكَ لَآيَةً لَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ يحتمل أن يكون من كلام نبيهم، أو من كلام الحق تعالى لنبينا- عليه الصلاة والسلام-.\nالإشارة: من شأن غالب النفوس ألا تقبل الخصوصية عند أحد حتى تظهر علامتُها، ولذلك طالب الكفارُ الرسلَ بالمعجزات، وطالب العوامُ الأولياءَ بالكرامات، ويكفي في الولي استقامة ظاهره، وتحقيق اليقين في باطنه.\nقال الشيخ أبو الحسن رضي الله عنه: «إنما هما كرامتان جامعتان محيطتان: كرامة الإيمان بمزيد الإيقان ونعت العيان، وكرامة العمل على السنة والمتابعة، وترك الدعاوى والمخادعة، فَمنْ أُعطيهما ثم جعل يشتاق إلى غيرهما فهو مفتر كذاب، أو ذو خطأ في العلم والعمل ... » الخ كلامه رضي الله عنه.\nوقال في العوارف: وقد يكون مَنْ لا يُكَاشَفُ بشيء من معاني القَدَر أفضل ممن يكاشف بها، إذا كاشفه الله تعالى بصرف المعرفة، فالقدرة أثر من القادر، ومن أُهِّل لقرب القادر لا يستغرب ولا يستكثر شيئاً من القدرة، ويرى القدرة تتجلّى من سُحُب أجزاء عالم الحكمة. فالكرامة إنما تظهر للقلوب المضطربة والنفوس المتزلزلة، وأما من سكن قلبه باليقين واطمأنت نفسه بالعيان لم يحتج إلى دليل ولا برهان إذ الجبال الراسية لا تحتاج إلى دُعامة، والله تعالى أعلم.\nوكل من طالب أهلَ الخصوصية بالكرامة الحسية ففيه نزعة إسرائيلية، حيث قالوا لنبيهم بعد أن عيَّن لهم مَنْ أكرمه الله بخصوصية الملك: (أَنَّى يَكُونُ لَهُ الملك عَلَيْنَا وَنَحْنُ أَحَقُ بِالْمُلْكِ منه) . ورد الحق تعالى عليهم بقوله:\nوَاللَّهُ يُؤْتِي مُلْكَهُ مَنْ يَشاءُ. وما أظهر لهم كرامة التابوت إلا بعد امتناعهم من الجهاد المُتَعَيَّنِ عليهم رحمةٌ بهم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم كمّل قصة خروجهم إلى العدو، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقالَ لَهُمْ نَبِيُّهُمْ إِنَّ آيَةَ مُلْكِهِ أَنْ يَأْتِيَكُمُ التَّابُوتُ فِيهِ سَكِينَةٌ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ وَبَقِيَّةٌ مِمَّا تَرَكَ آلُ مُوسى وَآلُ هارُونَ تَحْمِلُهُ الْمَلائِكَةُ إِنَّ فِي ذلِكَ لَآيَةً لَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (248)","quran_en":"248. Their prophet said to them, “The sign of his kingship is that the Ark will come to you, bearing tranquillity from your Lord and a remnant of what the family of Moses and the family of Aaron left behind, carried by the angels. In that is indeed a sign for you, if you are believers.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The form of the word tābūt","html":"<p>I say: al-Jawharī said that the original form of <i>tābūt<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التابوت<\/bdi>) is <i>tābuwa<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تابوة<\/bdi>), like <i>tarquwa<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ترقوة<\/bdi>), on the pattern <i>faʿluwa<\/i>. When the <i>wāw<\/i> was made quiescent, the feminine <i>hāʾ<\/i> became a <i>tāʾ<\/i>. Quraysh’s usage has <i>tāʾ<\/i>, and the Helpers’ usage <i>hāʾ<\/i>.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-202\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B191]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: al-Jawharī said that the original form of tābūt ( التابوت ) is tābuwa ( تابوة ), like tarquwa ( ترقوة ), on the pattern faʿluwa . When the wāw was made quiescent, the feminine hāʾ became a tāʾ . Quraysh’s usage has tāʾ , and the Helpers’ usage hāʾ . [B191]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The return of the Ark","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when they asked for proof that Ṭālūt had been chosen as king, their prophet told them: “The sign of his kingship is that the Ark will come to you.” It was a gilded chest of boxwood, three cubits long and two cubits broad. “In it is tranquillity from your Lord”: that through which your hearts find rest and remain firm in battle. They would place it before them in warfare, not flee, and receive victory over their enemy.<\/p><p>It is said that it contained representations of the prophets from Adam to Muḥammad. Another account says that it contained a gold basin in which the prophets’ hearts had been washed, and that this was the tranquillity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السكينة<\/bdi>). It also contained a remnant of what Moses’ family left: fragments of the Tablets, Moses’ staff and clothes, and Aaron’s turban. “Family” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">آل<\/bdi>) is inserted in both expressions.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-203\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B191]<\/a><\/p><p>“The angels carry it.” Wahb said: when the Ark came into the possession of the people who had defeated Israel, they put it in a sanctuary containing idols. Each morning the idols were found broken. They carried it to another people’s village, but pains afflicted that people. They said: “This is only because of this Ark; let us return it to Israel.” They placed it on a cart, harnessed two cows to it and sent them towards Israel’s land. God sent angels to drive the cows until they entered among the Israelites while these were occupied with Ṭālūt’s affair. They then became certain of victory. Other accounts are also given.<\/p><p>“In that is indeed a sign for you, if you are believers” may be the words of their prophet, or the words of the Real Most High to our Prophet.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when they asked for proof that Ṭālūt had been chosen as king, their prophet told them: “The sign of his kingship is that the Ark will come to you.” It was a gilded chest of boxwood, three cubits long and two cubits broad. “In it is tranquillity from your Lord”: that through which your hearts find rest and remain firm in battle. They would place it before them in warfare, not flee, and receive victory over their enemy. It is said that it contained representations of the prophets from Adam to Muḥammad. Another account says that it contained a gold basin in which the prophets’ hearts had been washed, and that this was the tranquillity ( السكينة ). It also contained a remnant of what Moses’ family left: fragments of the Tablets, Moses’ staff and clothes, and Aaron’s turban. “Family” ( آل ) is inserted in both expressions. [B191] “The angels carry it.” Wahb said: when the Ark came into the possession of the people who had defeated Israel, they put it in a sanctuary containing idols. Each morning the idols were found broken. They carried it to another people’s village, but pains afflicted that people. They said: “This is only because of this Ark; let us return it to Israel.” They placed it on a cart, harnessed two cows to it and sent them towards Israel’s land. God sent angels to drive the cows until they entered among the Israelites while these were occupied with Ṭālūt’s affair. They then became certain of victory. Other accounts are also given. “In that is indeed a sign for you, if you are believers” may be the words of their prophet, or the words of the Real Most High to our Prophet."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The greatest wonders are certainty and faithful practice","html":"<p>Most selves will not acknowledge spiritual election (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخصوصية<\/bdi>) in someone until its sign appears. Hence the unbelievers demanded miracles from the messengers, and ordinary people demand extraordinary gifts (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كرامات<\/bdi>) from saints. Yet the saint’s outward uprightness and inward realization of certainty suffice.<\/p><p>Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan said: “There are only two comprehensive, all-embracing extraordinary gifts: the gift of faith, through increased certainty and the quality of direct vision; and the gift of action in accordance with the Sunna and following the Prophet, abandoning claims and deception. Someone given these two who then longs for something else is a fabricating liar, or mistaken in knowledge and action …”—to the end of his words.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-204\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B192]<\/a><\/p><p>In the <i>ʿAwārif<\/i> it is said: someone to whom nothing of the meanings of destiny (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القدر<\/bdi>) is unveiled may be better than one to whom they are unveiled, if God unveils to him pure gnosis. Power (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القدرة<\/bdi>) is an effect of the Powerful (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القادر<\/bdi>). One made fit for nearness to the Powerful finds nothing in power strange or excessive; he sees power disclosing itself through the clouds formed by the parts of the world of wisdom. Extraordinary gifts appear only for unsettled hearts and wavering selves. Someone whose heart rests in certainty and whose self is tranquil through direct vision needs neither evidence nor proof. Firmly rooted mountains need no supporting prop. And God Most High knows best.<\/p><p>Everyone who demands a sensory wonder from people of spiritual election has an Israelite tendency in him. After their prophet had identified the person whom God honoured with the distinction of kingship, they said: “How can he have kingship over us when we have a stronger right to kingship than he?” The Real Most High answered them: “God grants His kingship to whom He wills.” He showed them the wonder of the Ark only after they refused the fighting incumbent upon them, as a mercy to them. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Most selves will not acknowledge spiritual election ( الخصوصية ) in someone until its sign appears. Hence the unbelievers demanded miracles from the messengers, and ordinary people demand extraordinary gifts ( كرامات ) from saints. Yet the saint’s outward uprightness and inward realization of certainty suffice. Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan said: “There are only two comprehensive, all-embracing extraordinary gifts: the gift of faith, through increased certainty and the quality of direct vision; and the gift of action in accordance with the Sunna and following the Prophet, abandoning claims and deception. Someone given these two who then longs for something else is a fabricating liar, or mistaken in knowledge and action …”—to the end of his words. [B192] In the ʿAwārif it is said: someone to whom nothing of the meanings of destiny ( القدر ) is unveiled may be better than one to whom they are unveiled, if God unveils to him pure gnosis. Power ( القدرة ) is an effect of the Powerful ( القادر ). One made fit for nearness to the Powerful finds nothing in power strange or excessive; he sees power disclosing itself through the clouds formed by the parts of the world of wisdom. Extraordinary gifts appear only for unsettled hearts and wavering selves. Someone whose heart rests in certainty and whose self is tranquil through direct vision needs neither evidence nor proof. Firmly rooted mountains need no supporting prop. And God Most High knows best. Everyone who demands a sensory wonder from people of spiritual election has an Israelite tendency in him. After their prophet had identified the person whom God honoured with the distinction of kingship, they said: “How can he have kingship over us when we have a stronger right to kingship than he?” The Real Most High answered them: “God grants His kingship to whom He wills.” He showed them the wonder of the Ark only after they refused the fighting incumbent upon them, as a mercy to them. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then completes the story of their march towards the enemy.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then completes the story of their march towards the enemy."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C275.1","page":275,"ar":"رضاض الشيء: كساره وفتاته.","en":"Ruḍāḍ of a thing: its broken pieces and fragments.","anchor":"رُضاض الألواح"}],"english_characters":3949,"arabic_characters":2933},{"id":"q2-249","q":"2:249","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:249 · The river’s test and the thirst of worldly desire","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 276–279","cairo":"I:276–279","source_page_first":276,"source_page_last":279,"ar":"فَلَمَّا فَصَلَ طالُوتُ بِالْجُنُودِ قالَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ مُبْتَلِيكُمْ بِنَهَرٍ فَمَنْ شَرِبَ مِنْهُ فَلَيْسَ مِنِّي وَمَنْ لَمْ يَطْعَمْهُ فَإِنَّهُ مِنِّي إِلاَّ مَنِ اغْتَرَفَ غُرْفَةً بِيَدِهِ فَشَرِبُوا مِنْهُ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً مِنْهُمْ فَلَمَّا جاوَزَهُ هُوَ وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا مَعَهُ قالُوا لا طاقَةَ لَنَا الْيَوْمَ بِجالُوتَ وَجُنُودِهِ قالَ الَّذِينَ يَظُنُّونَ أَنَّهُمْ مُلاقُوا اللَّهِ كَمْ مِنْ فِئَةٍ قَلِيلَةٍ غَلَبَتْ فِئَةً كَثِيرَةً بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ مَعَ الصَّابِرِينَ (249)\nقلت: قال في القاموس: غرف الماء يغرفه: أخذه بيده، كاغْتَرَفه، والغَرْفَةُ للمَرَّة، وبالكسر: هيئة الغرف وبالضم: اسم للمفعول، كالغرافة، لأنك ما لم تغرفه لا تسميه غُرْفَة، ثم قال: والغُرْفَةُ، بالضم: العُلِّيَّة(1) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ولما اتفقوا على مُلك طالوت تجهز للخروج، وقال: لا يخرج معه إلا الشبابُ النشيط الفارغُ ليس وراءه علقة(2) ، فاجتمع ممن اختار ثمانون ألفا، وقيل: ثلاثون، فلما انفصل عن بلده بالجنود وساروا في البيداء، - وكان وقت الحرِّ والقيّظ- عطشوا، وسألوا طالوتَ أن يُجري لهم نهراً، فقال لهم بوحْي، أو بإلهام، أو بأمر نبيهم: إِنَّ اللَّهَ مُبْتَلِيكُمْ أي: مُختبركم بِنَهَرٍ بسبب اقتراحكم، فَمَنْ شَرِبَ مِنْهُ كَرْعاً بلا واسطة فَلَيْسَ مِنِّي أي: من جيشي، وَمَنْ لَمْ يَطْعَمْهُ أي: يَذْقه، فَإِنَّهُ مِنِّي إِلَّا مَنِ اغْتَرَفَ غُرْفَةً بِيَدِهِ فإنها تكفيه لنفسه ولفرسه، فالاستثناء من الجملة الأولى.\nفَشَرِبُوا مِنْهُ أي: كَرعُوا، وسقطوا على وجوههم، إِلَّا قَلِيلًا مِنْهُمْ ثلاثُمائةٍ وأربعةَ عشر، على عدد أهل بدر، وقيل: ألفاً. رُوِيَ أن من اقتصر على الغَرفة كَفَتْه لشربه ودوابه، ومن لم يقتصر غلب عطشُه، واسودَّتْ شفتُه ولم يقدْر أن يمضيَ. وعن ابن عباس: أن القومَ شربوا على قدر يقينهم: فالكفار شربوا شُربَ الهيم، وشَرِب العاصي دون ذلك، وانصرف من القوم ستةٌ وسبعون ألفاً، وبقي بعضُ المؤمنين لم يشرب شيئاً، وأخذ بعضهم الغَرفة، فأما من شرب فاشتد به العطشُ وسقط، وأما من ترك الماءَ فحسن حالُه، وكان أجلَد ممن أخذ الغرفة. هـ.\nوحكمة هذا الامتحان: ليتخلص للجهاد المطيعون المخلصون، إذ لا يقع النصر إلاَّ بهم، فلما جاوز النهرَ طالوتُ ومن بقيَ معه ممن لم يشربْ قال بعضهم لبعض: لا طاقَةَ لَنَا الْيَوْمَ بِجالُوتَ وَجُنُودِهِ لكثرتهم وقلة عددنا، قالَ الَّذِينَ يَظُنُّونَ أي: يَتَيَقَّنْون أَنَّهُمْ مُلاقُوا اللَّهِ ويتوقعون ثوابَ الشهادة وهم الخُلْصُ من أهل البصيرة: لا تفزعوا من كثرة عددهم كَمْ مِنْ فِئَةٍ قَلِيلَةٍ غَلَبَتْ فِئَةً كَثِيرَةً بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ وإرادته ومعونته، و «كم» للتكثير، وَاللَّهُ مَعَ الصَّابِرِينَ بالنصر والمعونة.\nالإشارة: قال بعض الحكماء: الدنيا كنهر طالوت، لا ينجو منها إلا من لم يشربْ أو اغترف غرفةً بيده، فمن أخذ منها قَدْرَ الضرورةِ كَفَتْه، ونَشَطَ لعبادة مولاه، ومن أخذ فوق الحاجة حُبس في سجنها، وكان أسيرا فى يدها.\nوقال بعضهم: طالبُ الدنيا كشارب ماءِ البحر، كلما زاد شربه ازداد عطشه. هـ. وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من أُشرب قلبه حُبَ الدنيا التاط(1) منها بثلاث: بشغل لا ينفد عناه، وأمل لا يبلغ منتهاه، وحرص لا يدرك مداه» وقال عيسى عليه السلام: الدنيا مزرعة لإبليس، وأهلها حراث له. هـ. وقال على رضي الله عنه: الدنيا كالحية: لَيِّن مسها، قاتل سمها، فكن احذر ما تكونُ منها، أَسَرَّ ما تكون بها فإن من سكن منها إلى إيناس أزاله عنها إيحاش.\nوقال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «مِنْ هوان الدنيا على الله أنه لا يُعصى إلا فيها، ولا ينال ما عنده إلا بتركها» .\nوقال سيدنا علي- كرّم الله وجهه-: أول الدنيا عناء، وآخرها فناء، حلالها حساب، وحرامها عقاب، ومتشابهها عتاب، من استغنى فيها فُتن، ومن افتقر فيها حزن. هـ. وقيل: الدنيا تُقبل إقبال الطالب، وتُدبر إدبار الهارب، وتصل وصال الملول، وتُفارق فراق العجول، خيرها يسير، وعمرها قصير، ولذاتها فانية، وتبعاتها باقية.\nوقال عيسى عليه السلام: تعملون للدنيا، وأنتم تُرزقون فيها بغير عمل، ولا تعملون للآخرة وأنتم لا ترزقون فيها إلا بالعمل. هـ. وقيل: أوحى الله إلى الدنيا: مَنْ خدَمني فاخدِميه، ومن خدمك فاستخدِميه.\nوكان عمرُ بنُ عبد العزيز يتمثل بهذه الأبيات(2) :\nنهارك يا مغرورُ سهوٌ وغفلةٌ ... وليلُكَ نومٌ، والأسَى لك لازمٌ\nتُسَرُّ بما يفْنَى، وتفرحُ بالمُنَى ... كما سُرَّ باللذَّات في النومِ حالمُ\nوشغلُك فيها سوف تكرَه غَبَّه ... كذلك في الدنيا تَعيِشُ البهائمُ\nوقال آخر(3) :\nهي الدارُ دَارُ الأذى والقذى ... ودارُ الفناءِ ودارُ الْغِيَرْ\nفلو نِلْتَها بحذافيرها ... لمِتَّ ولم تقض منها الوطرْ\nأيا مَنْ يؤملُ طولَ الخلودِ ... وطولُ الخلودِ عليه ضررْ\nإذا ما كبِرْتَ وفات الشبَابُ ... فلا خيرَ في العيش بعد الكبر\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى قصة جالوت وملك داود عليه السلام، فقال:","quran_ar":"فَلَمَّا فَصَلَ طالُوتُ بِالْجُنُودِ قالَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ مُبْتَلِيكُمْ بِنَهَرٍ فَمَنْ شَرِبَ مِنْهُ فَلَيْسَ مِنِّي وَمَنْ لَمْ يَطْعَمْهُ فَإِنَّهُ مِنِّي إِلاَّ مَنِ اغْتَرَفَ غُرْفَةً بِيَدِهِ فَشَرِبُوا مِنْهُ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً مِنْهُمْ فَلَمَّا جاوَزَهُ هُوَ وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا مَعَهُ قالُوا لا طاقَةَ لَنَا الْيَوْمَ بِجالُوتَ وَجُنُودِهِ قالَ الَّذِينَ يَظُنُّونَ أَنَّهُمْ مُلاقُوا اللَّهِ كَمْ مِنْ فِئَةٍ قَلِيلَةٍ غَلَبَتْ فِئَةً كَثِيرَةً بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ مَعَ الصَّابِرِينَ (249)","quran_en":"249. When Ṭālūt set out with the forces, he said, “God will test you with a river. Whoever drinks from it is not of me; whoever does not taste it is of me—except one who scoops a single handful.” Yet they drank from it, except a few. When he and those who believed with him had crossed it, they said, “We have no strength today against Jālūt and his forces.” Those who expected to meet God said, “How many a small company has overcome a large company by God’s leave! God is with the steadfast.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Gharfa, ghirfa and ghurfa","html":"<p>I say: the <i>Qāmūs<\/i> states: <i>gharafa al-māʾa yaghrifuhu<\/i> means taking water with one’s hand, as does <i>ightarafahu<\/i>. <i>Gharfa<\/i>, with <i>fatḥa<\/i>, denotes a single act; <i>ghirfa<\/i>, with <i>kasra<\/i>, the manner of scooping; and <i>ghurfa<\/i>, with <i>ḍamma<\/i>, what is scooped, like <i>ghurāfa<\/i>, for before it is scooped one does not call it a <i>ghurfa<\/i>. It then says that <i>ghurfa<\/i>, with <i>ḍamma<\/i>, also means an upper chamber.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the Qāmūs states: gharafa al-māʾa yaghrifuhu means taking water with one’s hand, as does ightarafahu . Gharfa , with fatḥa , denotes a single act; ghirfa , with kasra , the manner of scooping; and ghurfa , with ḍamma , what is scooped, like ghurāfa , for before it is scooped one does not call it a ghurfa . It then says that ghurfa , with ḍamma , also means an upper chamber."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The testing of the army","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when they agreed to Ṭālūt’s kingship, he prepared to march, saying that only active young men, free from responsibilities and without ties left behind, should accompany him. Eighty thousand of those he selected assembled; another account says thirty thousand.<\/p><p>When he departed his town with the forces and they travelled through the wilderness, in the heat of summer, they became thirsty and asked him to make a river flow for them. By revelation, inspiration or his prophet’s command, he told them: “God will test you with a river,” because of your request. “Whoever drinks from it”—lapping directly without an intervening vessel—“is not of me,” not of my army. “Whoever does not taste it is of me—except one who scoops a handful,” which will suffice him and his horse. The exception refers back to the first clause.<\/p><p>They drank from it, lapping and falling upon their faces, except a few: three hundred and fourteen, corresponding to the people of Badr; or, according to another account, a thousand. It is related that those who restricted themselves to one handful found it sufficient for themselves and their animals. Those who did not became overwhelmed by thirst, their lips blackened, and they could not continue.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-205\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B193]<\/a><\/p><p>Ibn ʿAbbās is reported to have said that they drank according to the measure of their certainty. The unbelievers drank like thirst-stricken camels, and the disobedient less than that. Seventy-six thousand turned back. Some believers remained who drank nothing, while others took a handful. Those who drank became intensely thirsty and collapsed; those who left the water fared well and were stronger than those who took a handful.<\/p><p>The wisdom of this test was to single out the obedient and sincere for the struggle, since victory comes only through them. When Ṭālūt and those remaining with him who had not drunk crossed the river, some said to others: “We have no strength today against Jālūt and his forces,” because they are many and we are few. Those certain that they would meet God, expecting the reward of martyrdom—the pure people of inward sight—said: “Do not be alarmed by their numbers. How many a small company has overcome a large company by God’s leave,” His will and assistance. <i>Kam<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كم<\/bdi>) expresses multitude here. God is with the steadfast through victory and assistance.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when they agreed to Ṭālūt’s kingship, he prepared to march, saying that only active young men, free from responsibilities and without ties left behind, should accompany him. Eighty thousand of those he selected assembled; another account says thirty thousand. When he departed his town with the forces and they travelled through the wilderness, in the heat of summer, they became thirsty and asked him to make a river flow for them. By revelation, inspiration or his prophet’s command, he told them: “God will test you with a river,” because of your request. “Whoever drinks from it”—lapping directly without an intervening vessel—“is not of me,” not of my army. “Whoever does not taste it is of me—except one who scoops a handful,” which will suffice him and his horse. The exception refers back to the first clause. They drank from it, lapping and falling upon their faces, except a few: three hundred and fourteen, corresponding to the people of Badr; or, according to another account, a thousand. It is related that those who restricted themselves to one handful found it sufficient for themselves and their animals. Those who did not became overwhelmed by thirst, their lips blackened, and they could not continue. [B193] Ibn ʿAbbās is reported to have said that they drank according to the measure of their certainty. The unbelievers drank like thirst-stricken camels, and the disobedient less than that. Seventy-six thousand turned back. Some believers remained who drank nothing, while others took a handful. Those who drank became intensely thirsty and collapsed; those who left the water fared well and were stronger than those who took a handful. The wisdom of this test was to single out the obedient and sincere for the struggle, since victory comes only through them. When Ṭālūt and those remaining with him who had not drunk crossed the river, some said to others: “We have no strength today against Jālūt and his forces,” because they are many and we are few. Those certain that they would meet God, expecting the reward of martyrdom—the pure people of inward sight—said: “Do not be alarmed by their numbers. How many a small company has overcome a large company by God’s leave,” His will and assistance. Kam ( كم ) expresses multitude here. God is with the steadfast through victory and assistance."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Take from the world only what is needed","html":"<p>One of the sages said: this world is like Ṭālūt’s river. Only someone who does not drink, or scoops a handful, escapes it. Whoever takes only what is necessary finds it sufficient and becomes energetic in worshipping his Master. Whoever takes more than he needs is confined in its prison and held captive in its hand.<\/p><p>Someone said: the seeker of the world resembles a drinker of seawater—the more he drinks, the thirstier he becomes. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Someone whose heart is made to drink love of the world becomes attached through it to three things: occupation whose toil never ends, hope whose limit is never reached, and eagerness whose utmost extent is never attained.” Jesus said: “The world is Iblīs’s field, and its people are his cultivators.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-206\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B194]<\/a><\/p><p>ʿAlī said: “The world is like a snake: soft to touch, deadly in its venom. Be most wary of it when most delighted by it. Whoever rests in it through familiarity is driven from it through estrangement.”<\/p><p>The Prophet is also quoted as saying: “A sign of the world’s insignificance before God is that He is disobeyed only within it, and what is with Him is attained only by abandoning it.” Our master ʿAlī, may God ennoble his face, said: “The world begins in toil and ends in passing away. Its lawful things entail an accounting; its unlawful things punishment; its doubtful things reproach. Whoever becomes wealthy in it is tried; whoever becomes poor in it grieves.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-207\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B194]<\/a><\/p><p>It is said: the world approaches like a seeker and retreats like a fugitive. It joins like someone easily bored, and separates like someone in haste. Its good is little, its life short, its pleasures passing and its consequences enduring.<\/p><p>Jesus said: “You work for this world, although you are provided for in it without work. You do not work for the Hereafter, although you will receive no provision there except through work.” It is also said that God revealed to the world: “Serve whoever serves Me, and make whoever serves you serve you.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-208\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B194]<\/a><\/p><p>ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz used to cite these verses:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Your day, deluded one, is heedlessness and neglect;<br>your night is sleep, while sorrow clings to you.<br>You delight in what passes and rejoice in wishes,<br>as a dreamer delights in pleasures during sleep.<br>The aftermath of your occupation with it you will hate:<br>so do the beasts live in this world.<\/blockquote><p>Another said:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">This abode is an abode of hurt and irritation,<br>an abode of passing away and change.<br>Even were you to obtain it in its entirety,<br>you would die without satisfying your desire for it.<br>You who hope for prolonged abiding—<br>prolonged abiding would harm you.<br>When you have aged and youth has passed,<br>there is no good in life after old age.<\/blockquote>","authorial":true,"text":"One of the sages said: this world is like Ṭālūt’s river. Only someone who does not drink, or scoops a handful, escapes it. Whoever takes only what is necessary finds it sufficient and becomes energetic in worshipping his Master. Whoever takes more than he needs is confined in its prison and held captive in its hand. Someone said: the seeker of the world resembles a drinker of seawater—the more he drinks, the thirstier he becomes. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Someone whose heart is made to drink love of the world becomes attached through it to three things: occupation whose toil never ends, hope whose limit is never reached, and eagerness whose utmost extent is never attained.” Jesus said: “The world is Iblīs’s field, and its people are his cultivators.” [B194] ʿAlī said: “The world is like a snake: soft to touch, deadly in its venom. Be most wary of it when most delighted by it. Whoever rests in it through familiarity is driven from it through estrangement.” The Prophet is also quoted as saying: “A sign of the world’s insignificance before God is that He is disobeyed only within it, and what is with Him is attained only by abandoning it.” Our master ʿAlī, may God ennoble his face, said: “The world begins in toil and ends in passing away. Its lawful things entail an accounting; its unlawful things punishment; its doubtful things reproach. Whoever becomes wealthy in it is tried; whoever becomes poor in it grieves.” [B194] It is said: the world approaches like a seeker and retreats like a fugitive. It joins like someone easily bored, and separates like someone in haste. Its good is little, its life short, its pleasures passing and its consequences enduring. Jesus said: “You work for this world, although you are provided for in it without work. You do not work for the Hereafter, although you will receive no provision there except through work.” It is also said that God revealed to the world: “Serve whoever serves Me, and make whoever serves you serve you.” [B194] ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz used to cite these verses: Your day, deluded one, is heedlessness and neglect; your night is sleep, while sorrow clings to you. You delight in what passes and rejoice in wishes, as a dreamer delights in pleasures during sleep. The aftermath of your occupation with it you will hate: so do the beasts live in this world. Another said: This abode is an abode of hurt and irritation, an abode of passing away and change. Even were you to obtain it in its entirety, you would die without satisfying your desire for it. You who hope for prolonged abiding— prolonged abiding would harm you. When you have aged and youth has passed, there is no good in life after old age."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then relates the story of Jālūt and the kingship of David.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then relates the story of Jālūt and the kingship of David."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C277.1","page":277,"ar":"العلية بضم العين وكسرها: هي الغرفة في الطبقة الثانية من الدار وما فوقها، وجمعها (علالي).","en":"ʿUliyya, also pronounced with kasra on the ʿayn (ʿiliyya), is a room on the second level of a house or above it; its plural is ʿalālī.","anchor":"العُلِّيَّة"},{"id":"C277.2","page":277,"ar":"أي: ما يتعلق به وجمعها علق. وذلك كتجارة، وزوجة لم يدخل بها، وغير ذلك.","en":"That is, something to which he is attached; its plural is ʿulaq. Examples include trade, a wife with whom he has not yet consummated the marriage, and other such things.","anchor":"علقة"},{"id":"C278.1","page":278,"ar":"التاط: أي التصق.","en":"Iltāṭa: that is, adhered.","anchor":"التاط"},{"id":"C278.2","page":278,"ar":"الأبيات لمسعر بن كدام، كما في حلية الأولياء ٧/٢٢٠.","en":"The verses are by Misʿar ibn Kidām, as recorded in Ḥilyat al-awliyāʾ, 7:220.","anchor":"وكان عمرُ بنُ عبد العزيز يتمثل بهذه الأبيات"},{"id":"C278.3","page":278,"ar":"وهو أبو العتاهية.","en":"He is Abū al-ʿAtāhiya.","anchor":"وقال آخر"}],"english_characters":5494,"arabic_characters":4140},{"id":"q2-250-252","q":"2:250–252","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:250–252 · David’s victory and the good through which corruption is restrained","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 279–282","cairo":"I:279–282","source_page_first":279,"source_page_last":282,"ar":"وَلَمَّا بَرَزُوا لِجالُوتَ وَجُنُودِهِ قالُوا رَبَّنا أَفْرِغْ عَلَيْنا صَبْراً وَثَبِّتْ أَقْدامَنا وَانْصُرْنا عَلَى الْقَوْمِ الْكافِرِينَ (250) فَهَزَمُوهُمْ بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ وَقَتَلَ داوُدُ جالُوتَ وَآتاهُ اللَّهُ الْمُلْكَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَعَلَّمَهُ مِمَّا يَشاءُ وَلَوْلا دَفْعُ اللَّهِ النَّاسَ بَعْضَهُمْ بِبَعْضٍ لَفَسَدَتِ الْأَرْضُ وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ ذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى الْعالَمِينَ (251) تِلْكَ آياتُ اللَّهِ نَتْلُوها عَلَيْكَ بِالْحَقِّ وَإِنَّكَ لَمِنَ الْمُرْسَلِينَ (252)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ولما برز طالوت بمَن معه لِجالُوتَ، أي: ظهر في البرَاز، ودنا بعضهم من بعض، تضرعوا إلى الله واستنصروه، وقالوا: رَبَّنا أَفْرِغْ عَلَيْنا صَبْراً أي: أصْبُبْه علينا صبّاً، وَثَبِّتْ أَقْدامَنا عند اللقاء لئلا نَفِرّ، وَانْصُرْنا عَلَى الْقَوْمِ الْكافِرِينَ. وفي دعائهم ترتيب بليغ سألوا أولاً إفراغ الصبر في قلوبهم الذي هو ملاك الأمر، ثم ثبات القدمِ في مَدَاحِضِ الحرب المُسببِ عنه، ثم النصرَ على العدو المرتب عليها غالباً.\nفهزم الله عدوَّهم وأجاب دعاءهم بإذنه وقدرته، وَقَتَلَ داوُدُ جالُوتَ. وقصة قتله: أن أصحاب طالوت كان فيهم بنو إيش، وهو أبو داود عليه السلام ستة أو سبعة، وكان داود صغيراً يرعى غنماً، فلما حضرت الحرب قال فى نفسه: لأذهبن لرؤية هذه الحرب، فمرَّ في طريقه، بحجر فناداه: يا داود خُذْني، فبي تقتل جالوت، ثم ناداه حجر آخر ثم آخر فأخذها، وجعلها في مخلاته وسار، فلما حضر البأس خرج جالوت يطلب البراز، وكاع الناس عنه(1)، أي: تأخروا خوفاً، حتى قال طالوت: من يبرز له ويقتله فأنا أزوجه ابنتي، وأُحَكِّمُهُ في مالي، فجاء داود، فقال له طالوت: اركب فرسي وخذ سلاحي، ففعل، وخرج في أحسن شكله، فلما مشى قليلاً رجع، فقال الناس: جَبُنَ الفتى، فقال داود: إن الله سبحانه لم يقتله ولم يُعنى عليه، لم ينفعني هذا الفرس ولا هذا السلاح، ولكني أحب أن أقاتله\nعلى عادتي. وكان داود من أرمى الناس بالمقلاع، فنزل، وأخذ مخلاته فتقلدها، وأخذ مقلاعه فخرج إلى جالوت، وهو شاكٍ في السلاح(1)، فقال جالوت: أنت يا فتى تخرج إليّ! قال، نعم، قال: هكذا كما تخرج إلى الكلب! قال:\nنعم، وأنت أهون، قال: لأطعمن لحمك اليوم الطير والسباع، ثم تدانيا فأدار داود فأخذ مقلاعه وأدخل يده إلى الحجارة، فروى أنها التأمت، وصارت حجراً واحداً، فأخذه ووضعه في المقلاع، وسَمَّى الله، وأداره، ورماه، فأصاب رأس جالوت فقتله، وجزّ رأسه، وجعله في مخلاته، واختلط الناس، وحمل أصحابُ طالوت فكانت الهزيمة.\nثم إن داود جاء يطلب شرطه من طالوت، فقال: حتى تقتل مائتين من هؤلاء الجراجمة(2) الذين يؤذون الناس وتجيئني بسلبهم، فقتل داود منهم مائتين، وجاء بذلك، فدفع إليه امرأته وتخلّى له عن الملك(3) . ولما تمكن داود- عليه السلام- من الملك، أجلى من بقي من قوم جالوت إلى المغرب، فمن بقيتهم البرابرة من الشلوح وسائر الأرياف.\nفآتى اللهُ داود الْمُلْكَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وهي النبوة، وقيل: صنعة الدروع ومنطق الطير وَعَلَّمَهُ مِمَّا يَشاءُ من أنواع العلوم والمعارف والأسرار، وقد دفع الله بأس الكافرين ورد كيدهم في نحرهم، وَلَوْلا دَفْعُ اللَّهِ النَّاسَ بَعْضَهُمْ بِبَعْضٍ لَفَسَدَتِ الْأَرْضُ أي: لولا أنَّ الله يدفع بعض الناس ببعض، فينصر المسلمين على الكافرين، ويكف فسادهم، لغلبوا وأفسدوا في الأرض. أو: لولا أن الله نصب السلطان، وأقام الحكام لينصفوا المظلوم من الظالم، ويردوا القوي عن الضعيف، لتواثب الخلق بعضهم على بعض، وأكل القوي الضعيفَ فيفسد النظام. أو: لولا أن الله يدفع بالشهود عن الناس في حفظ الأموال والنفوس والدماء والأعراض، لوقع الفساد في الأرض.\nأو: لولا أن الله يدفع بأهل الطاعة والإحسان عن أهل الغفلة والعصيان، لفسدت الأرض بشؤم أهل العصيان.\nوفى الخبر عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «إنَّ الله يَدْفَعُ بالمُصلِّي مِنْ أمَّتِي عَمَّنْ لا يصلى، وبمن يُزكيَّ عَمَّنْ لاَ يُزَكِّي، وبمن يصوم،\nعَمَّنْ لاَ يَصُوم، وبمَنْ يَحُجُّ، عَمَّنْ لاَ يَحُجُّ، وبَمَّنْ يُجاهِدُ عَمَّنْ لاَ يُجَاهِدُ. وَلَوِ اجْتَمَعُوا عَلَى تَرْكِ هذِهِ الأشْياءِ ما أنْظَرَهُمُ الله طَرْفة عَيْنٍ» .\nوفي حديث آخر: «لولا عباد لله رُكَّع، وصبية رُضَّع، لصبَّ عَليكُمُ العَذَابُ صَبًّا» . ورَوى جابر رضي الله عنه قال: قال رسول الله صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «إن الله ليُصْلِحُ بصلاح الرجل- ولده وولد ولدهِ، وأهل دُوَيْرَتِه، ودويراتٍ حوله، ولا يزالون في حفظ الله مادام فيهم» . هـ. فهذا من فضل الله على عباده يصلح طالحهم بصالحهم، ويُشَفع خيارُهُم في شرارهم، ولولا ذلك لعوجلوا بالهلاك، وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ ذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى الْعالَمِينَ.\nتِلْكَ يا محمد، آياتُ اللَّهِ والإشارة إلى ما قصَّ من حديث الألوف، وتمليك طالوت، وإتيان التابوت، وانهزام الجبابرة أصحاب جالوت، نَتْلُوها أي: نقصها عليكم بِالْحَقِّ أي: بالوجه المطابق الذي لا يَشُك فيه أهل الكتاب وأرباب التواريخ، وَإِنَّكَ لَمِنَ الْمُرْسَلِينَ حيث أخبرتَ بها من غير تعرف ولا استماع ولم يعهد منك تعلم ولا اطلاع، فلا يشك أنه مِنْ عند الخبير العليم، إلا من طَبع اللهُ على قلبه. نعوذ بالله من ذلك.\nالإشارة: «من علامة النجح في النهايات الرجوع إلى الله في البدايات» ، فإذا برز المريد لجهاد أعدائه من النفس وَالهوى والشيطان وسائر القُطاع، واستنصر بالله وتبرأ من حوله وقوته، كان ذلك علامة على نصره وظفره بنفسه، وكان سبباً في نجح نهايته، فيملكه الله الوجود بأسره، ويفتح عليه من خزائن حكمته. قال أبو سليمان الداراني: (إذا اعتادت النفوس على ترك الآثام، جالت في الملكوت ثم عادت إلى صاحبها بطرائف الحكم من غير أن يؤدي إليها عالم علماً) . وفي الخبر: «من عَمِلَ بِمَا عَلِمَ أَوْرَثَهُ اللهُ علمَ مَا لم يعلمْ» . وكان حينئذٍ رحمة للعباد، يدفع الله بوجوده العذاب عمن يستحقه من عباده.\nوفي الحديث القدسي: «يقول الله عزّ وجلّ: إذا كان الغالبَ على عبدي الاشتغالُ بي جعلتُ همته ولذَّته في ذكري، ورفعتُ الحجابُ فيما بيني وبينه، لا يسهو إذا سَهَا الناسُ، أولئك كَلاَمُهُم كلام الأنبياءِ، أولئك الأبطالُ حقًا، أولئك الذين إذا أردتُ بأهلِ الأرضِ عقوبةً أو عذابًا ذكرتُهم فصرفتُه بهم عنهم» . حقَّقنا الله بمحبتهم وجعلنا منهم.. آمين.\nولمَّا ذكر في هذه السورة جملة من الأنبياء والرسل، وشهد لرسوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم أنه من المرسلين ذكر تفضيل بعضهم على بعض في الجملة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلَمَّا بَرَزُوا لِجالُوتَ وَجُنُودِهِ قالُوا رَبَّنا أَفْرِغْ عَلَيْنا صَبْراً وَثَبِّتْ أَقْدامَنا وَانْصُرْنا عَلَى الْقَوْمِ الْكافِرِينَ (250) فَهَزَمُوهُمْ بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ وَقَتَلَ داوُدُ جالُوتَ وَآتاهُ اللَّهُ الْمُلْكَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَعَلَّمَهُ مِمَّا يَشاءُ وَلَوْلا دَفْعُ اللَّهِ النَّاسَ بَعْضَهُمْ بِبَعْضٍ لَفَسَدَتِ الْأَرْضُ وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ ذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى الْعالَمِينَ (251) تِلْكَ آياتُ اللَّهِ نَتْلُوها عَلَيْكَ بِالْحَقِّ وَإِنَّكَ لَمِنَ الْمُرْسَلِينَ (252)","quran_en":"250. When they came forth against Jālūt and his forces, they said, “Our Lord, pour steadfastness upon us, make our feet firm, and grant us victory over the unbelieving people.” 251. They routed them by God’s leave, and David killed Jālūt. God gave him kingship and wisdom and taught him of whatever He willed. Were God not to repel some people by means of others, the earth would be corrupted; but God is bountiful towards all worlds. 252. These are God’s signs, which We recite to you in truth; and you are indeed one of the messengers.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The ordered prayer for victory","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when Ṭālūt and those with him came forth against Jālūt, appearing in the open ground and drawing near one another, they humbly supplicated God and sought His victory: “Our Lord, pour steadfastness upon us”—pour it forth abundantly—“make our feet firm” at the encounter, lest we flee, “and grant us victory over the unbelieving people.”<\/p><p>Their supplication has an eloquent order. First they ask that steadfastness be poured into their hearts, since it is the foundation of the affair; then for firmness of foot in the slippery places of battle, which results from it; then for victory over the enemy, which ordinarily follows these two. God routed their enemy and answered their prayer by His leave and power.<\/p><p class=\"translator-note-ref\">[Translator’s print-source notes: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/R07-11\">[R07-11]<\/a>]<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when Ṭālūt and those with him came forth against Jālūt, appearing in the open ground and drawing near one another, they humbly supplicated God and sought His victory: “Our Lord, pour steadfastness upon us”—pour it forth abundantly—“make our feet firm” at the encounter, lest we flee, “and grant us victory over the unbelieving people.” Their supplication has an eloquent order. First they ask that steadfastness be poured into their hearts, since it is the foundation of the affair; then for firmness of foot in the slippery places of battle, which results from it; then for victory over the enemy, which ordinarily follows these two. God routed their enemy and answered their prayer by His leave and power. [Translator’s print-source notes: [R07-11] ]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"David and Jālūt","html":"<p>“David killed Jālūt.” The account of his killing is this. Among Ṭālūt’s companions were six or seven sons of Īsh, David’s father. David was a young boy tending sheep. When battle approached, he said to himself: “I shall go and see this battle.” On his way he passed a stone, which called: “David, take me; through me you will kill Jālūt.” Another stone called, then another. He took them, put them in his bag and continued.<\/p><p>When the fighting began, Jālūt came out demanding single combat. The men <i>kāʿū<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كاعوا<\/bdi>): they drew back in fear. Ṭālūt said: “Whoever goes out to him and kills him, I shall marry to my daughter and give authority over my property.” David came forward. Ṭālūt told him to mount his horse and take his arms. He did so and went out in splendid form. After a short distance, however, he returned. The men said: “The youth has lost courage.” David replied: “Unless God Most High kills him and assists me against him, this horse and arms will not benefit me. I prefer to fight him<\/p><p>in my customary manner.”<\/p><p>David was among the most skilful men with a sling. He dismounted, slung his bag over himself, took his sling and went out against Jālūt, who was fully armed. Jālūt said: “You, boy, come out against me?” He replied: “Yes.” “Like this, as you would go out against a dog?” “Yes—and you are more contemptible.” Jālūt said: “Today I shall feed your flesh to birds and beasts.”<\/p><p>They drew near. David took his sling and reached for the stones. It is related that they joined into a single stone. He put it into the sling, named God, swung it and released it. It struck Jālūt’s head and killed him. He cut off his head and put it in his bag. The forces became intermingled, Ṭālūt’s companions charged, and the rout followed.<\/p><p>David then asked Ṭālūt to fulfil his promise. He replied: “Not until you kill two hundred of these Jarājima who harm people and bring me their spoils.” David killed two hundred and brought them. Ṭālūt gave him his bride and relinquished kingship to him. When David was established as king, he expelled the remaining people of Jālūt to the Maghrib. The Berbers—the Shlūḥ and the inhabitants of the other rural regions—are said here to be their remnant.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-209\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B195]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“David killed Jālūt.” The account of his killing is this. Among Ṭālūt’s companions were six or seven sons of Īsh, David’s father. David was a young boy tending sheep. When battle approached, he said to himself: “I shall go and see this battle.” On his way he passed a stone, which called: “David, take me; through me you will kill Jālūt.” Another stone called, then another. He took them, put them in his bag and continued. When the fighting began, Jālūt came out demanding single combat. The men kāʿū ( كاعوا ): they drew back in fear. Ṭālūt said: “Whoever goes out to him and kills him, I shall marry to my daughter and give authority over my property.” David came forward. Ṭālūt told him to mount his horse and take his arms. He did so and went out in splendid form. After a short distance, however, he returned. The men said: “The youth has lost courage.” David replied: “Unless God Most High kills him and assists me against him, this horse and arms will not benefit me. I prefer to fight him in my customary manner.” David was among the most skilful men with a sling. He dismounted, slung his bag over himself, took his sling and went out against Jālūt, who was fully armed. Jālūt said: “You, boy, come out against me?” He replied: “Yes.” “Like this, as you would go out against a dog?” “Yes—and you are more contemptible.” Jālūt said: “Today I shall feed your flesh to birds and beasts.” They drew near. David took his sling and reached for the stones. It is related that they joined into a single stone. He put it into the sling, named God, swung it and released it. It struck Jālūt’s head and killed him. He cut off his head and put it in his bag. The forces became intermingled, Ṭālūt’s companions charged, and the rout followed. David then asked Ṭālūt to fulfil his promise. He replied: “Not until you kill two hundred of these Jarājima who harm people and bring me their spoils.” David killed two hundred and brought them. Ṭālūt gave him his bride and relinquished kingship to him. When David was established as king, he expelled the remaining people of Jālūt to the Maghrib. The Berbers—the Shlūḥ and the inhabitants of the other rural regions—are said here to be their remnant. [B195]"},{"type":"theology","title":"The means by which God repels corruption","html":"<p>God gave David kingship and wisdom: prophecy; or, according to another explanation, the craft of making coats of mail and the speech of birds. He taught him of whatever He willed: kinds of sciences, gnoses and mysteries. God repelled the might of the unbelievers and turned their plotting back upon them.<\/p><p>“Were God not to repel some people by means of others, the earth would be corrupted.” Were He not to give Muslims victory over unbelievers and restrain their corruption, the latter would prevail and spread corruption on earth. Alternatively, were God not to establish rulers and judges to give the oppressed justice against the oppressor and restrain the strong from the weak, people would spring at one another, the strong consume the weak, and the order of things collapse.<\/p><p>Or, were God not to protect property, lives, blood and honour through witnesses, corruption would arise on earth. Or, were God not to repel affliction from the heedless and disobedient through the people of obedience and goodness, the earth would be corrupted through the ill effects of disobedience.<\/p><p>A report from the Prophet says: “Through those of my community who pray, God repels affliction from those who do not pray; through those who give alms, from those who do not give alms; through those who fast,<\/p><p>from those who do not fast; through those who make pilgrimage, from those who do not; and through those who fight in His cause, from those who do not. Were they all to abandon these things, God would not grant them respite for the blink of an eye.” Another report says: “Were it not for servants of God bowing in prayer and infants suckling, punishment would be poured upon you abundantly.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-210\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B196]<\/a><\/p><p>Jābir related that the Messenger of God said: “Through a man’s righteousness, God sets right his child, his child’s child, the people of his little household and the households around him. They remain in God’s protection so long as he is among them.” This is God’s bounty towards His servants: He sets right their corrupt through their righteous and accepts the intercession of their best for their worst. Otherwise destruction would be hastened upon them. “But God is bountiful towards all worlds.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-211\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B196]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God gave David kingship and wisdom: prophecy; or, according to another explanation, the craft of making coats of mail and the speech of birds. He taught him of whatever He willed: kinds of sciences, gnoses and mysteries. God repelled the might of the unbelievers and turned their plotting back upon them. “Were God not to repel some people by means of others, the earth would be corrupted.” Were He not to give Muslims victory over unbelievers and restrain their corruption, the latter would prevail and spread corruption on earth. Alternatively, were God not to establish rulers and judges to give the oppressed justice against the oppressor and restrain the strong from the weak, people would spring at one another, the strong consume the weak, and the order of things collapse. Or, were God not to protect property, lives, blood and honour through witnesses, corruption would arise on earth. Or, were God not to repel affliction from the heedless and disobedient through the people of obedience and goodness, the earth would be corrupted through the ill effects of disobedience. A report from the Prophet says: “Through those of my community who pray, God repels affliction from those who do not pray; through those who give alms, from those who do not give alms; through those who fast, from those who do not fast; through those who make pilgrimage, from those who do not; and through those who fight in His cause, from those who do not. Were they all to abandon these things, God would not grant them respite for the blink of an eye.” Another report says: “Were it not for servants of God bowing in prayer and infants suckling, punishment would be poured upon you abundantly.” [B196] Jābir related that the Messenger of God said: “Through a man’s righteousness, God sets right his child, his child’s child, the people of his little household and the households around him. They remain in God’s protection so long as he is among them.” This is God’s bounty towards His servants: He sets right their corrupt through their righteous and accepts the intercession of their best for their worst. Otherwise destruction would be hastened upon them. “But God is bountiful towards all worlds.” [B196]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The recitation of the signs and the proof of messengership","html":"<p>“These, Muḥammad, are God’s signs.” The reference is to the account of the thousands, Ṭālūt’s appointment as king, the Ark’s arrival and the defeat of the tyrants who followed Jālūt. We recite—that is, relate—them to you in truth: in a corresponding account about which the People of the Book and possessors of historical knowledge have no doubt. You are indeed one of the messengers, since you have reported them without investigation or hearing them from others; no study or acquaintance with such matters was previously known of you. None doubts that this comes from the All-Aware, the Knowing, except one whose heart God has sealed. We seek God’s protection from that.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-212\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B195]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“These, Muḥammad, are God’s signs.” The reference is to the account of the thousands, Ṭālūt’s appointment as king, the Ark’s arrival and the defeat of the tyrants who followed Jālūt. We recite—that is, relate—them to you in truth: in a corresponding account about which the People of the Book and possessors of historical knowledge have no doubt. You are indeed one of the messengers, since you have reported them without investigation or hearing them from others; no study or acquaintance with such matters was previously known of you. None doubts that this comes from the All-Aware, the Knowing, except one whose heart God has sealed. We seek God’s protection from that. [B195]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Return to God at the beginning, and serve as a mercy at the end","html":"<p>“A sign of success at the end is returning to God at the beginning.” When the disciple comes forth to struggle against his enemies—the self, desire, Satan and the other highwaymen—and seeks God’s assistance, disavowing his own power and strength, this is a sign of his victory and triumph over his self, and a cause of success at his journey’s end. Through God he gains mastery over all existence, and the treasuries of His wisdom are opened to him.<\/p><p>Abū Sulaymān al-Dārānī said: “When selves become accustomed to abandoning sins, they roam through the kingdom of the unseen (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الملكوت<\/bdi>), then return to their owner with rare wisdoms, without any scholar having communicated knowledge to them.” A report says: “Whoever acts upon what he knows, God bequeaths to him knowledge of what he did not know.” He then becomes a mercy to the servants: through his existence, God repels punishment from those of His servants who deserve it.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-213\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B197]<\/a><\/p><p>In a sacred report, God, mighty and majestic, is quoted as saying: “When occupation with Me predominates in My servant, I place his aspiration and delight in My remembrance and raise the veil between Me and him. He is not heedless when people are heedless. Their speech is the speech of prophets; they are the true champions. When I intend a punishment or affliction for the people of the earth, I remember them and turn it away from the people through them.” May God establish us in their love and make us among them. Amen.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-214\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B197]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"“A sign of success at the end is returning to God at the beginning.” When the disciple comes forth to struggle against his enemies—the self, desire, Satan and the other highwaymen—and seeks God’s assistance, disavowing his own power and strength, this is a sign of his victory and triumph over his self, and a cause of success at his journey’s end. Through God he gains mastery over all existence, and the treasuries of His wisdom are opened to him. Abū Sulaymān al-Dārānī said: “When selves become accustomed to abandoning sins, they roam through the kingdom of the unseen ( الملكوت ), then return to their owner with rare wisdoms, without any scholar having communicated knowledge to them.” A report says: “Whoever acts upon what he knows, God bequeaths to him knowledge of what he did not know.” He then becomes a mercy to the servants: through his existence, God repels punishment from those of His servants who deserve it. [B197] In a sacred report, God, mighty and majestic, is quoted as saying: “When occupation with Me predominates in My servant, I place his aspiration and delight in My remembrance and raise the veil between Me and him. He is not heedless when people are heedless. Their speech is the speech of prophets; they are the true champions. When I intend a punishment or affliction for the people of the earth, I remember them and turn it away from the people through them.” May God establish us in their love and make us among them. Amen. [B197]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having mentioned a number of prophets and messengers in this sūrah and testified that His Messenger is one of them, He speaks in general of the preference given to some over others.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having mentioned a number of prophets and messengers in this sūrah and testified that His Messenger is one of them, He speaks in general of the preference given to some over others."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C279.1","page":279,"ar":"كاع فلان: جبن وضعف.","en":"Kāʿa fulān: the man became cowardly and weak.","anchor":"وكاع الناس عنه"},{"id":"C280.1","page":280,"ar":"يقال: رجل شاكي السلاح: تام التسلح.","en":"One says rajul shākī al-silāḥ of a fully armed man.","anchor":"شاكٍ في السلاح"},{"id":"C280.2","page":280,"ar":"الجراجمة: قوم من العجم بالجزيرة. ويقال: الجراجمة نبط الشام.","en":"The Jarājima: a non-Arab people in al-Jazīra. It is also said that the Jarājima are the Nabataeans of Syria.","anchor":"الجراجمة"},{"id":"C280.3","page":280,"ar":"هذا القصص كله لين الأسانيد. كما قال ابن عطية. وقال الدكتور أبو شهبة: نحن في غنية عن هذا القصص بما في أيدينا من القرآن والسنة، ولسنا في حاجة إلى شيء من هذا في فهم القرآن وتدبره. انظر الإسرائيليات والموضوعات للدكتور أبي شهبة، رحمه الله.","en":"All these stories have weak chains of transmission, as Ibn ʿAṭiyya said. Dr Abū Shahba said: “We have no need for these stories, given the Quran and Sunna in our possession; we need none of this to understand and reflect upon the Quran.” See Dr Abū Shahba’s al-Isrāʾīliyyāt wa-l-mawḍūʿāt; may God have mercy on him.","anchor":"وتخلّى له عن الملك"}],"english_characters":7512,"arabic_characters":5396},{"id":"q2-253","q":"2:253","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:253 · The messengers’ distinctions and the mystery of human disagreement","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 282–284","cairo":"I:282–284","source_page_first":282,"source_page_last":284,"ar":"تِلْكَ الرُّسُلُ فَضَّلْنا بَعْضَهُمْ عَلى بَعْضٍ مِنْهُمْ مَنْ كَلَّمَ اللَّهُ وَرَفَعَ بَعْضَهُمْ دَرَجاتٍ وَآتَيْنا عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ الْبَيِّناتِ وَأَيَّدْناهُ بِرُوحِ الْقُدُسِ ...\nقلت: (تلك) : مبتدأ، و (الرسل) : نعت، أو بدل منه، أو بيان، و (فضلنا) ، خبر، أو (الرسل) خبر، و (فضلنا) : خبر ثان، والإشارة إلى الجماعة المذكور قَصَصُها في السورة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: تِلْكَ الرُّسُلُ الذين قصصناهم عليك، وذكرتُ لك أنك منهم، فَضَّلْنا بَعْضَهُمْ عَلى بَعْضٍ بخصائص ومناقب لم توجد في غيره. لكن هذا التفضيل إنما يكون في الجملة من غير تعيين المفضول، لأنه تنقيص في حقه وهو ممنوع. ولذلك قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لا تُخَيِّرُوا بَيْنَ الأَنبِيَاءَ» ، «ولا تفضلوني على يونس بن متى» فإن معناه النهي عن تعيين المفضول، لأنه غيبة وتنقيص، وقد صرح صلّى الله عليه وسلم بفضله على جميع الأنبياء بقوله: «أنَا سَيِّدُ وَلَدِ آدَمَ ولا فخر» . لكن لا يُعَيِّن أحداً من الأنبياء بالمفضولية لئلا يؤدي إلى نقصه، فلا تعارُضَ بين الحديثين.\nمِنْهُمْ مَنْ كَلَّمَ اللَّهُ وهو موسى عليه السلام في جبل الطور، وسيدنا محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم حين كان قاب قوسين أو أدنى، وَرَفَعَ بَعْضَهُمْ دَرَجاتٍ وهو نبيّنا محمد صلّى الله عليه وسلم فإنه خُصَّ بالدعوة العامة، والحُجَج المتكاثرة، والمعجزات المستمرة، والآيات المتعاقبة بتعاقب الدهر، والفضائل العلمية والعملية الفائتة للحصر. والإبهام لتفخيم شأنه، كأنه العلم المشهور المتعين لهذا الوصف المستغني عن التعيين. وقيل: إبراهيم، خصه بالخلة التي هي أعلى المراتب.\nقلت: بل المحبة أعلى منها(1) ، وقيل: إدريس لقوله: وَرَفَعْناهُ مَكاناً عَلِيًّا، وقيل: أولو العزم من الرسل، قاله البيضاوي.\nوَآتَيْنا عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ الْبَيِّناتِ أي: الآيات الواضحات، كإحياء الموتى، وإبراء الأكمه والأبرص، (وأيدناه بروح القدس) ، أي: جبريل عليه السلام كان معه أينما سار، وخصَّه بالتعيين لإفراط اليهود والنصارى في تحقيره وتعظيمه، فردَّهم إلى الصواب باعتقاد نبوته دون ربوبيته.\nالإشارة: كما فضَّل الله الرسل بعضهم على بعض، كذلك فضل الأولياء بعضهم على بعض، وإنما يقع التفضيل بكمال اليقين، والتغلغل في علم التوحيد الخاص، ذوقاً وكشفاً، والترقي في المعارف والأسرار. وذلك بخدمة الرجال وصحبة أهل الكمال، والتفرغ التام، والزهد الكامل في النفس والفَلْس والجنْس، فمنهم من تحصل له المشاهدة وتصحبها المكالمة، ومنهم من تحصل له المشاهدة دون المكالمة، ومنهم من تحصل له الكرامات الواضحة، ومنهم من لا يرى شيئاً من ذلك استغناءً عنها بكرامة المعرفة. وما قيل في الرسل من عدم تعيين المفضول، مثله يقال في حق الأولياء، وإلا وقع في الغيبة الشنيعة فإن لحوم الأولياء سموم، فليعتقد الكمال في الجميع، ولا يصرح بتعيين المفضول كما تقدّم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولما ذكر الحقّ تعالى أحوال الرسل، وتفاوتهم في العناية، ذكر أحوال أممهم وتفاوتهم في الهداية، فقال:\n... وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ مَا اقْتَتَلَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ بَعْدِهِمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ وَلكِنِ اخْتَلَفُوا فَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ آمَنَ وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ كَفَرَ وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ مَا اقْتَتَلُوا وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ يَفْعَلُ مَا يُرِيدُ (253)\nقلت: إذا وقع فعل المشيئة بعد (لو) فالغالب حذف مفعوله، كقوله: وَلَوْ شِئْنا لَرَفَعْناهُ بِها، أي: لو شئنا رفعه لرفعناه بها، وكقوله: وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ مَا اقْتَتَلَ ... ، أي: لو شاء هدايتهم ما اقتتلوا، وغير ذلك.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ولما بعثتُ الرسلَ، وفضَّلتُ بعضَهم على بعض، اختلفت أممهم من بعدهم فاقتتلوا، وكل ذلك بإرادتي ومشيئتي، وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ هداية أممهم مَا اقْتَتَلَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ بَعْدِهِمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ المعجزات الواضحات في تحقيق رسالتهم وصحة نبوتهم، وَلكِنِ اخْتَلَفُوا بغياً وحسداً فَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ آمَنَ بتوفيقه لاتباع دين الأنبياء، وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ كَفَرَ بمخالفتهم، فكان من الأشقياء، وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ جمعهم على الهدى مَا اقْتَتَلُوا، لكن حكمته اقتضت وجود الاختلاف ليظهر سر اسمه المنتقم والقهار واسمه الكريم والحليم، وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ يَفْعَلُ ما يُرِيدُ لاَ يُسْئَلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْئَلُونَ.\nوفي الآية دليل على أن الحوادث كلها بيد الله خيرها وشرها، وأن أفعال العباد كلها بقدرته تعالى، لا تأثير لشيء من الكائنات فيها. وهذا يردُّ قول المعتزلة القائلين بخلق العبد أفعاله، فما أبعدهم عن الله. نسأل الله العصمة بمنِّه وكرمه.\nالإشارة: اختلاف الناس على الأولياء سُنة ماضية وحكمة أزلية، وَلَنْ تَجِدَ لِسُنَّةِ اللَّهِ تَبْدِيلًا، وَلا يَزالُونَ مُخْتَلِفِينَ، فمن رأيته من الأولياء اتفق الناسُ على تعظيمه في حياته فهو ناقص أو جاهل بالله إذ\nالداخل على الله منكور، والراجع إلى الناس مبرور، وهذا هو الغالب، والنادر لا حكم له، فلو كان الاتفاق محموداً لكان على الأنبياء أولى، فلما لم يقع للأنبياء والرسل، لم يقع للأولياء إذ هم على قدمهم، وقائمون بالوراثة الكاملة عنهم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم حضّ على الصدقة فى سبيل الله لأنها برهان الإيمان وعنوان الهداية، فقال:","quran_ar":"تِلْكَ الرُّسُلُ فَضَّلْنا بَعْضَهُمْ عَلى بَعْضٍ مِنْهُمْ مَنْ كَلَّمَ اللَّهُ وَرَفَعَ بَعْضَهُمْ دَرَجاتٍ وَآتَيْنا عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ الْبَيِّناتِ وَأَيَّدْناهُ بِرُوحِ الْقُدُسِ وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ مَا اقْتَتَلَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ بَعْدِهِمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ وَلكِنِ اخْتَلَفُوا فَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ آمَنَ وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ كَفَرَ وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ مَا اقْتَتَلُوا وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ يَفْعَلُ مَا يُرِيدُ (253)","quran_en":"253. Those messengers: We favoured some over others. Among them are those to whom God spoke, and He raised some by degrees. We gave Jesus son of Mary the clear proofs and strengthened him with the Holy Spirit. Had God willed, those who came after them would not have fought one another after the clear proofs came to them. But they differed: some believed and some disbelieved. Had God willed, they would not have fought one another; but God does whatever He desires.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The demonstrative and its predicate","html":"<p>I say: “Those” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تلك<\/bdi>) is the subject; “messengers” is its adjective, substitute or explanatory apposition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعت، بدل، بيان<\/bdi>); and “We favoured” is the predicate. Alternatively, “messengers” is the predicate and “We favoured” a second predicate. The demonstrative refers to the company whose stories have been mentioned in the sūrah.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Those” ( تلك ) is the subject; “messengers” is its adjective, substitute or explanatory apposition ( نعت، بدل، بيان ); and “We favoured” is the predicate. Alternatively, “messengers” is the predicate and “We favoured” a second predicate. The demonstrative refers to the company whose stories have been mentioned in the sūrah."},{"type":"theology","title":"Preference without disparagement","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those messengers whom We have related to you, and among whom I have said that you belong—We favoured some over others through distinctive gifts and virtues not found in another. This preference, however, is expressed generally, without identifying a particular prophet as inferior, since that diminishes him and is forbidden. Hence the Prophet said: “Do not make distinctions of superiority among the prophets,” and, “Do not prefer me over Jonah son of Mattā.” These prohibit identifying someone as inferior, which is backbiting and diminution.<\/p><p>The Prophet nevertheless stated his superiority over all the prophets: “I am the master of the children of Adam, without boasting.” But no individual prophet is to be designated inferior, lest this lead to diminishing him. Thus the two reports do not conflict.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-215\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B198]<\/a><\/p><p>“Among them are those to whom God spoke”: Moses upon Mount Ṭūr, and our master Muḥammad when he was “two bows’ lengths away or nearer.” “He raised some by degrees”: our Prophet Muḥammad, distinguished by a universal mission, abundant proofs, enduring miracles, signs succeeding one another throughout time, and virtues of knowledge and action beyond enumeration. Leaving him unnamed magnifies his standing, as though he were the well-known figure uniquely marked by this description and needing no explicit identification.<\/p><p>Another explanation identifies Abraham, distinguished by intimate friendship (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخلة<\/bdi>), said to be the highest rank. I say: rather, love (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحبة<\/bdi>) is higher than it. Another identifies Idrīs, because of “We raised him to a lofty place” [19:57]; another, the messengers of firm resolve (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أولو العزم<\/bdi>). Al-Bayḍāwī gives these explanations.<\/p><p>“We gave Jesus son of Mary the clear proofs”: manifest signs, such as raising the dead and healing the person born blind and the leper. “We strengthened him with the Holy Spirit”: Gabriel, who accompanied him wherever he went. He is explicitly named because of the Jews’ and Christians’ excesses in belittling and magnifying him. God returns them to the right course: belief in his prophethood, not his Lordship.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those messengers whom We have related to you, and among whom I have said that you belong—We favoured some over others through distinctive gifts and virtues not found in another. This preference, however, is expressed generally, without identifying a particular prophet as inferior, since that diminishes him and is forbidden. Hence the Prophet said: “Do not make distinctions of superiority among the prophets,” and, “Do not prefer me over Jonah son of Mattā.” These prohibit identifying someone as inferior, which is backbiting and diminution. The Prophet nevertheless stated his superiority over all the prophets: “I am the master of the children of Adam, without boasting.” But no individual prophet is to be designated inferior, lest this lead to diminishing him. Thus the two reports do not conflict. [B198] “Among them are those to whom God spoke”: Moses upon Mount Ṭūr, and our master Muḥammad when he was “two bows’ lengths away or nearer.” “He raised some by degrees”: our Prophet Muḥammad, distinguished by a universal mission, abundant proofs, enduring miracles, signs succeeding one another throughout time, and virtues of knowledge and action beyond enumeration. Leaving him unnamed magnifies his standing, as though he were the well-known figure uniquely marked by this description and needing no explicit identification. Another explanation identifies Abraham, distinguished by intimate friendship ( الخلة ), said to be the highest rank. I say: rather, love ( المحبة ) is higher than it. Another identifies Idrīs, because of “We raised him to a lofty place” [19:57]; another, the messengers of firm resolve ( أولو العزم ). Al-Bayḍāwī gives these explanations. “We gave Jesus son of Mary the clear proofs”: manifest signs, such as raising the dead and healing the person born blind and the leper. “We strengthened him with the Holy Spirit”: Gabriel, who accompanied him wherever he went. He is explicitly named because of the Jews’ and Christians’ excesses in belittling and magnifying him. God returns them to the right course: belief in his prophethood, not his Lordship."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Degrees among the saints","html":"<p>As God favoured some messengers over others, He favoured some saints over others. The distinction lies in perfected certainty, penetration into the special knowledge of unity through taste and unveiling, and ascent in gnoses and mysteries. It comes through serving the men of the path, accompanying people of perfection, complete freedom for the pursuit, and full renunciation of self, money and kindred attachments (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النفس والفلس والجنس<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Some attain witnessing together with divine address (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المكالمة<\/bdi>); some attain witnessing without address; some receive manifest extraordinary gifts; and some see none of these, having no need of them because of the gift of gnosis. What was said about not identifying an inferior among the messengers applies likewise to the saints. Otherwise one falls into dreadful backbiting: the flesh of saints is poisonous. Let one believe in the perfection of them all and not explicitly designate anyone inferior, as already explained. And God Most High knows best.<\/p><p>The Real Most High, having mentioned the messengers’ states and their differing degrees of divine care, then mentions their communities and their differing degrees of guidance.<\/p><div class=\"quranpanel embedded-quran\" data-q=\"2:253b\"><div class=\"meta\">Qurʾānic continuation · 2:253b<\/div><div class=\"quranar\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ مَا اقْتَتَلَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ بَعْدِهِمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ وَلكِنِ اخْتَلَفُوا فَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ آمَنَ وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ كَفَرَ وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ مَا اقْتَتَلُوا وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ يَفْعَلُ ما يُرِيدُ<\/div><div class=\"quranen\">Had God willed, those who came after them would not have fought one another after the clear proofs came to them. But they differed: some believed and some disbelieved. Had God willed, they would not have fought one another; but God does whatever He desires.<\/div><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"As God favoured some messengers over others, He favoured some saints over others. The distinction lies in perfected certainty, penetration into the special knowledge of unity through taste and unveiling, and ascent in gnoses and mysteries. It comes through serving the men of the path, accompanying people of perfection, complete freedom for the pursuit, and full renunciation of self, money and kindred attachments ( النفس والفلس والجنس ). Some attain witnessing together with divine address ( المكالمة ); some attain witnessing without address; some receive manifest extraordinary gifts; and some see none of these, having no need of them because of the gift of gnosis. What was said about not identifying an inferior among the messengers applies likewise to the saints. Otherwise one falls into dreadful backbiting: the flesh of saints is poisonous. Let one believe in the perfection of them all and not explicitly designate anyone inferior, as already explained. And God Most High knows best. The Real Most High, having mentioned the messengers’ states and their differing degrees of divine care, then mentions their communities and their differing degrees of guidance. Qurʾānic continuation · 2:253b وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ مَا اقْتَتَلَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ بَعْدِهِمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ وَلكِنِ اخْتَلَفُوا فَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ آمَنَ وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ كَفَرَ وَلَوْ شاءَ اللَّهُ مَا اقْتَتَلُوا وَلكِنَّ اللَّهَ يَفْعَلُ ما يُرِيدُ Had God willed, those who came after them would not have fought one another after the clear proofs came to them. But they differed: some believed and some disbelieved. Had God willed, they would not have fought one another; but God does whatever He desires."},{"type":"language","title":"The omitted object of willing","html":"<p>I say: when a verb of willing follows <i>law<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لو<\/bdi>), its object is usually omitted. Thus “Had We willed, We would have raised him through them” [7:176] means: had We willed to raise him, We would have raised him through them. Likewise, “Had God willed, they would not have fought one another” means: had He willed their guidance, they would not have fought—and similarly elsewhere.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: when a verb of willing follows law ( لو ), its object is usually omitted. Thus “Had We willed, We would have raised him through them” [7:176] means: had We willed to raise him, We would have raised him through them. Likewise, “Had God willed, they would not have fought one another” means: had He willed their guidance, they would not have fought—and similarly elsewhere."},{"type":"theology","title":"Disagreement, divine will and the manifestation of the Names","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when I sent the messengers and favoured some over others, their communities differed after them and fought. All this occurred through My will and desire. Had God willed the guidance of their communities, those who came after them would not have fought after receiving the clear miracles establishing their messengership and authenticating their prophethood. But they differed through transgression and envy. Some believed, through His granting them success in following the prophets’ religion; some disbelieved by opposing them and became among the wretched.<\/p><p>Had God willed to unite them upon guidance, they would not have fought. His wisdom, however, required disagreement to exist so that the mystery of His Names the Avenger (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المنتقم<\/bdi>) and the Subduer (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القهار<\/bdi>), and the Generous (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكريم<\/bdi>) and the Forbearing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحليم<\/bdi>), might appear. God does whatever He desires. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they are questioned” [21:23].<\/p><p>The verse proves that all events, good and evil, are in God’s hand, and that all the servants’ acts occur through His power. Nothing created exercises efficacy within them. This refutes the Muʿtazilites’ assertion that the servant creates his acts. How far they are from God! We ask God for protection through His grace and generosity.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-216\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B199]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when I sent the messengers and favoured some over others, their communities differed after them and fought. All this occurred through My will and desire. Had God willed the guidance of their communities, those who came after them would not have fought after receiving the clear miracles establishing their messengership and authenticating their prophethood. But they differed through transgression and envy. Some believed, through His granting them success in following the prophets’ religion; some disbelieved by opposing them and became among the wretched. Had God willed to unite them upon guidance, they would not have fought. His wisdom, however, required disagreement to exist so that the mystery of His Names the Avenger ( المنتقم ) and the Subduer ( القهار ), and the Generous ( الكريم ) and the Forbearing ( الحليم ), might appear. God does whatever He desires. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they are questioned” [21:23]. The verse proves that all events, good and evil, are in God’s hand, and that all the servants’ acts occur through His power. Nothing created exercises efficacy within them. This refutes the Muʿtazilites’ assertion that the servant creates his acts. How far they are from God! We ask God for protection through His grace and generosity. [B199]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Disagreement over the saints","html":"<p>People’s disagreement over saints is an established way and an eternal wisdom. “You will find no alteration in God’s way” [33:62]; “They do not cease to differ” [11:118]. Any saint you see whom everyone agrees to honour during his lifetime is deficient or ignorant of God, for<\/p><p>one entering upon God is denied, while one returning to people is honoured. This is the general rule; the rare exception does not determine the judgment. Were agreement praiseworthy, the prophets would have had the strongest claim to it. Since it did not occur for prophets and messengers, it does not occur for saints, who follow in their footsteps and stand in complete inheritance from them. And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-217\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B200]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"People’s disagreement over saints is an established way and an eternal wisdom. “You will find no alteration in God’s way” [33:62]; “They do not cease to differ” [11:118]. Any saint you see whom everyone agrees to honour during his lifetime is deficient or ignorant of God, for one entering upon God is denied, while one returning to people is honoured. This is the general rule; the rare exception does not determine the judgment. Were agreement praiseworthy, the prophets would have had the strongest claim to it. Since it did not occur for prophets and messengers, it does not occur for saints, who follow in their footsteps and stand in complete inheritance from them. And God Most High knows best. [B200]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then urges charity in God’s path, for it is a proof of faith and a sign of guidance.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then urges charity in God’s path, for it is a proof of faith and a sign of guidance."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C282.1","page":282,"ar":"سواء كانت المحبة أعلى أم الخلة، فكلاهما حاصلة لنبينا وسيدنا محمد صلّى الله عليه وسلم. وانظر في مسألة: أيهما أعلى: المحبة أم الخلة؟ الشفا للقاضي عياض ١/٢١٣.","en":"Whether love or intimate friendship is higher, both belong to our Prophet and master Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace. On the question of which is higher, love or intimate friendship, see al-Qāḍī ʿIyāḍ’s al-Shifāʾ, 1:213.","anchor":"بل المحبة أعلى منها"}],"english_characters":6663,"arabic_characters":4359},{"id":"q2-254","q":"2:254","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:254 · Spend before the day when worldly transactions cease","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 284–284","cairo":"I:284","source_page_first":284,"source_page_last":284,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا أَنْفِقُوا مِمَّا رَزَقْناكُمْ مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ يَأْتِيَ يَوْمٌ لا بَيْعٌ فِيهِ وَلا خُلَّةٌ وَلا شَفاعَةٌ وَالْكافِرُونَ هُمُ الظَّالِمُونَ (254)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا أَنْفِقُوا مِمَّا رَزَقْناكُمْ واجباً أو تطوعاً في وجوه الخير، وخصوصاً في الجهاد الذي نحن بصدد الحضّ عليه، وقدموا لأنفسكم ما تجدونه بعد موتكم مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ يَأْتِيَ يَوْمٌ الحساب، واقتضاء الثواب، يوم ليس فيه بَيْعٌ ولا شراء، فيكتسب ما يقع به الفداء، وليس فيه خُلَّةٌ تنفعُ إلا خلة الأتقياء، وَلا شَفاعَةٌ ترجى إِلَّا مَنْ أَذِنَ لَهُ الرَّحْمنُ وَرَضِيَ لَهُ قَوْلًا فأنفقوا مما خولناكم في سبيل الله، وجاهدوا الكافرين أعداء الله، فإن الكافرين هُمُ الظَّالِمُونَ حيث وضعوا عبادتهم في غير محلها، ونسبوا الربوبية لغير مستحقيها، إذ لا يستحقها إلا الحي القيوم، الذي أشار إليه الحق جلّ جلاله:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا أَنْفِقُوا مِمَّا رَزَقْناكُمْ مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ يَأْتِيَ يَوْمٌ لا بَيْعٌ فِيهِ وَلا خُلَّةٌ وَلا شَفاعَةٌ وَالْكافِرُونَ هُمُ الظَّالِمُونَ (254)","quran_en":"254. You who believe, spend from what We have provided you before a day comes in which there is neither bargaining, nor friendship, nor intercession. The unbelievers are the wrongdoers.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Prepare through giving before the accounting","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who believe, spend from what We have provided you, obligatorily or voluntarily, upon good causes—particularly the fighting in His cause that We are now urging. Send ahead for yourselves what you will find after death, before the day of accounting and the rendering of recompense arrives.<\/p><p>On that day there is no buying or selling through which one might acquire a ransom; no friendship avails except the friendship of the reverent; and no intercession may be hoped for except that of one to whom the All-Merciful gives permission and whose speech He approves. Spend, then, in God’s path from what We have entrusted to you, and struggle against the unbelievers, God’s enemies. The unbelievers are the wrongdoers: they have placed their worship where it does not belong and attributed Lordship to those unworthy of it. Only the Living, the Self-Subsisting, merits it, as the Real, glorious is His majesty, indicates in the following verse.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who believe, spend from what We have provided you, obligatorily or voluntarily, upon good causes—particularly the fighting in His cause that We are now urging. Send ahead for yourselves what you will find after death, before the day of accounting and the rendering of recompense arrives. On that day there is no buying or selling through which one might acquire a ransom; no friendship avails except the friendship of the reverent; and no intercession may be hoped for except that of one to whom the All-Merciful gives permission and whose speech He approves. Spend, then, in God’s path from what We have entrusted to you, and struggle against the unbelievers, God’s enemies. The unbelievers are the wrongdoers: they have placed their worship where it does not belong and attributed Lordship to those unworthy of it. Only the Living, the Self-Subsisting, merits it, as the Real, glorious is His majesty, indicates in the following verse."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":982,"arabic_characters":817},{"id":"q2-255","q":"2:255","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:255 · The Living, the Self-Subsisting: the Verse of the Footstool","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 284–288","cairo":"I:284–288","source_page_first":284,"source_page_last":288,"ar":"اللَّهُ لاَ إِلهَ إِلاَّ هُوَ الْحَيُّ الْقَيُّومُ لا تَأْخُذُهُ سِنَةٌ وَلا نَوْمٌ لَهُ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ مَنْ ذَا الَّذِي يَشْفَعُ عِنْدَهُ إِلاَّ بِإِذْنِهِ يَعْلَمُ ما بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَما خَلْفَهُمْ وَلا يُحِيطُونَ بِشَيْءٍ مِنْ عِلْمِهِ إِلاَّ بِما شاءَ وَسِعَ كُرْسِيُّهُ السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضَ وَلا يَؤُدُهُ حِفْظُهُما وَهُوَ الْعَلِيُّ الْعَظِيمُ (255)\nقلت: (الله) : مبتدأ، وجملة (لاَ إله إِلاَّ هُوَ) : خبره، والضمير المنفصل بدل من المستتر في الخبر، و (الحي) : إما خبر ثان، أو لمبتدأ مضمر، أو بدل من (الله) ، و (قيوم) فَيْعُول، مبالغة من القيام، ومعناه: القائم بنفسه المستغني عن غيره.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: اللَّهُ الواجب الوجود لا يستحق العبادة غيره، فمن عبد غيره فقد أتى بظلم عظيم الْحَيُّ أي: الدائم بلا أول، الباقي بلا زوال الذي لا سبيل عليه للموت والفناء، الْقَيُّومُ أي: دائم القيام بتدبير خلقه في إيصال المنافع ودفع المضار، وجلب الأرزاق وأنواع الارتقاء، لا تَأْخُذُهُ سِنَةٌ وَلا نَوْمٌ السنة:\nما يتقدم النوم من الفتور، والنوم: حالة تعرض للإنسان من استرخاء أعصاب الدماغ من رطوبات الأبخرة المتصاعدة، فتقف الحواس الظاهرة عن الإحساس رأساً.\nوتقديم السنَة عليه، على ترتيب الوجود، كقوله تعالى: وَلا يُنْفِقُونَ نَفَقَةً صَغِيرَةً وَلا كَبِيرَةً، وجمع بينهما لأنه لو اقتصر على نفى السنّة عنه لتوهم أن النوم يغلبه لأنه أشد، ولو اقتصر على نفي النوم لتوهم أن السنة تلحقه لخفتها. والمراد تنزيهه تعالى عن آفات البشرية، وتأكيد كونه حيّاً قيوماً، فإن من أخذه نعاس أو نوم يكون مؤوف الحياة(1)، قاصراً في الحفظ والتدبير. ولذلك ترك العطف فيه وفي الجمل التي بعدَه لأنها كلها مقررة له، أي: للحيّ للقيّوم.\nوقد ورد أنه اسم الله الأعظم، وقال عليه الصلاة والسلام لفاطمة- رضي الله عنها: «ما مَنَعك أن تَسْمَعي ما أُوصِيك به تَقُولين إذا أصْبَحْتِ وإذا أمْسَيتِ يا حيّ يا قيوم، برحمتِكَ أستغيث أصْلحْ لي شأني كُلَّه، ولا تَكلْني إلى نفْسِي طَرْفَةَ عَيْنِ» . رواه النسائي. وأخرج مسلم عن أبى موسى رضي الله عنه قال: «قَامَ فِينَا رسُولُ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بخَمْسِ كلماتٍ قال: إنَّ الله عزّ وجل لا ينامُ، ولا يَنْبَغِي لَهُ أنْ يَنَام، يَخْفِضُ القِسطَ ويَرْفَعُهُ. يُرْفَعُ إلَيْهِ عَمَلُ اللَّيل قَبْلَ عَمَلِ النَّهَارِ وعَمَلُ النَّهَارِ قَبْلَ عَملِ اللَّيلِ، حِجَابُهُ النُّورُ- وفي رواية. النَّارُ- لَوْ كَشَفَهُ لأحرقت سُبحات وجهه ما أدركه بَصَرُهُ مِنْ خَلْقِهِ» .\nلَهُ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ هذا تقرير لقيوميته تعالى، واحتجاج على تفرده في الألوهية.\nوالمراد بما فيهما: ما هو أعمُّ من أجزائهما الداخلة فيهما ومن الأمور الخارجة عنهما، المتمكنة فيهما، من العقلاء وغيرهم، فهو أبلغ من (له السموات والأرض وما فيهن) ، يعني: أن الله يملك جميع ذلك من غير شريك ولا منازع، وعبر ب- (ما) تغليبا للغالب.\nمَنْ ذَا الَّذِي يَشْفَعُ عِنْدَهُ إِلَّا بِإِذْنِهِ هذا بيان لكبرياء شأنه، وأنه لا يدانيه أحد ليقدر على تغيير ما يريده بشفاعة واستكانة، فضلاً عن أن يعاوقه عناداً أو مناصبة. والاستفهام إنكاري، أي: لا أحد يشفع عنده لمن أراد تعالى عقوبته، إلا بإذنه، وذلك أن المشركين زعموا أن الأصنام تشفع لهم، فأخبر تعالى أنه لا شفاعة عنده إلا بإذنه، يريد بذلك شفاعة النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم وبعض الأنبياء والأولياء والملائكة.\nيَعْلَمُ ما بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَما خَلْفَهُمْ أي: ما قبلهم وما بعدهم، أو بالعكس، لأنك تستقبل المستقبل وتستدبر الماضي وقيل: يَعْلَمُ ما بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ من الدنيا وَما خَلْفَهُمْ من الآخرة، وقيل: عكسه، لأنهم يقدمون ويُخَلِّفُون الدنيا وراءهم، وقيل: يعلم ما قدموه بين أيديهم من خير أو شر، وما خلفهم وما هم فاعلوه، أو عكسه.\nوالمراد أنه سبحانه أحاط بالأشياء كلها، فلا يخفى عليه شيء وَلا يُحِيطُونَ بِشَيْءٍ مِنْ عِلْمِهِ إِلَّا بِما شاءَ أي: لا يحيطون بشيء من معلوماته تعالى إلا بما شاء أن يُطْلعهم عليه، وعطفه على ما قبله لأن مجموعه يدل على تفرده تعالى بالعلم الذاتي التام، الدال على وحدانيته تعالى في ذاته وصفاته.\nوَسِعَ كُرْسِيُّهُ السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضَ يقال: فلان يسَعُ الشيء سعة إذا احتمله وأطاقه وأمكنه القيامُ به. ويقال:\nوسع الشيءُ الشيء إذا أحاط به وغمره حتى اضمحلّ في جانبه، وهذا المعنى هو اللائق هنا. وأصل الكرسي في اللغة: من تَركّب الشيء بعضه على بعض، ومنه الكراسة، لتركب أوراقها بعضها على بعض، وفي العرف: اسم لما يُقعد عليه، سُمِّي به لتركب خشباته. واختلف فيه فقيل: العرش، وقيل: غيره.\nوالصحيح أنه مخلوق عظيم أمام العرش، فوق السموات السبع دون العرش. يقال: إن السموات والأرض في جنب الكرسي كحلقة في فلاة. والكرسي في جانب العرش كحلقة في فلاة. وعن ابن عباس: (أن السموات في الكرسي كدراهم سبعة في تُرْسٍ) وقيل: كرسيه: علمه.\nقال البيضاوي: هو تصوير لعظمته تعالى وتمثيل مجرد، كقوله وَما قَدَرُوا اللَّهَ حَقَّ قَدْرِهِ وَالْأَرْضُ جَمِيعاً قَبْضَتُهُ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ وَالسَّماواتُ مَطْوِيَّاتٌ بِيَمِينِهِ ولا كرسي في الحقيقة ولا قاعد(1) . وقيل: كرسيه مجاز عن\nعلمه أو ملكه، مأخوذ من كرسي العلم والملك، وقيل: جسم بين يدي العرش محيط بالسماوات السبع لقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «ما السموات السبع والأرضون السبع في الكرسي إلا كحلقة في فلاة، وفضل العرش على الكرسي كفضل تلك الفلاة على تلك الحلقة» ولعله الفلك المشهور بفلك البروج. هـ. قلت: وقد اعترض السيوطي في حاشيته عليه(1) . فالله تعالى أعلم.\nوَلا يَؤُدُهُ أي: لا يُثْقله ولاَ يُشقُّ عليه حِفْظُهُما أي: حفظ السموات والأرض. وإنما لم يتعرض لذكر ما فيهما لأن حفظهما مُسْتَتْبع لحفظه، وَهُوَ الْعَلِيُّ أي: المتعالي عن الأشباه والأنداد، الْعَظِيمُ أي: عظيم الشأن، جليل القدر، الذي يستحقر كلُّ شيء دون عظمته.\nوهذه الآية مشتملة على أمهات المسائل الإلهية، فإنها دالّة على أنه تعالى موجود واحد في الألوهية، متصف بالحياة الذاتية، واجب الوجود لذاته، موجد لغيره إذ القيوم هو القائم بنفسه المقيم لغيره، منزه عن التحيز والحلول، مُبرَّأ عن التغير والفتور، لا يناسب الأشباح، ولا يعتريه ما يعتري الأرواح، مالك الملك والملكوت، مبدع الأصول والفروع، ذو البطش الشديد، الذي لا يشفع عنده إلا من أذن له. عالم بالأشياء كلها: جليّها وخفيّها، كلّيّها وجِزْئيَّها.\nواسع الملك والقدرة لكل ما يصح أن يملك ويقدر عليه، لا يشقُّ عليه شاقٌّ، ولا يشغله شأن عن شأن، مُتَعَالٍ عن تناول الأوهام، عظيمٌ لا تحيط به الأفهام، ولذلك تفردت عن أخواتها بفضائل رائعة وخواص فائقة، قال صلّى الله عليه وسلم:\n«أعظمُ آيةٍ في القرآنِ آية الكرسىّ» . وقال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «مَنْ قَرَأَ آيَةَ الكُرْسيّ دُبر كل صلاة مكتوبة لم يَمْنَعْهُ من دُخُولِ الْجنَّة إلا الموتُ- وفي رواية- كانَ الذي يَتوَلَّى قَبْضَ رُوحِه ذُو الجَلاَل والإكْرَام- ولا يُوَاظِبُ عليها إلا صِدِّيقٌ أو عَابدْ، ومن قَرَأها إذا أخَذ مَضْجَعَه أمن على نفْسِه وَجارِه وَجارِ جَارِه، والأبيات حوله» .\nوقال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «ما قُرئت هذه الآيةُ في بيْت إلا هَجَرَتْهُ الشياطينُ ثلاثين يَوْماً، ولا يدْخُله ساحرُ ولا ساحرةٌ أربعين يوماً، يا عليّ علِّمْها ولدَك وأهلكَ وجيرانَك، فما نزلَتْ آيةٌ أعظمُ منها» . قاله البيضاوي وأبو السعود، وتكلم السيوطي في بعض هذه الأحاديث. والفضائل يعمل فيها بالضعيف. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: يا أيها الذين آمنوا أيمان أهل الخصوصية- (أَنفِقُواْ مما رزقناكم) من سعة العلوم ومخازن الفهوم، من قبل أن يأتي يوم اللقاء، يوم تسقط فيه المعاملات وتغيب تلك الإشارات، لا ينفع فيه إلا الدخول من باب الكرم،\nفيلقى الله بالله دون شيء سواه، والجاحدون لهذا هم الظالمون لأنفسهم، حيث اعتمدوا على أعمالهم فلقُوا الله بالصنم الأعظم. والحيُّ القيوم الكبير المتعال غني عن الانتفاع بالأعمال. وبالله التوفيق.\nومن عرف أنه الحي الذي لا يموت توكل عليه. قال تعالى: وَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى الْحَيِّ الَّذِي لا يَمُوتُ. والتعلق به: استمداد حياة الروح بالعلم والمحبة الكاملة. ومن عرف أنه الحي القيّوم وثق به، ونسي ذكر كل شيء بذكره، ولم يشاهد غيره بمشاهدة قيوميته. والتعلق به استمداد معرفة قيوميته حتى يستريح من نكد التدبير، والتخلق به بأن تكون قائماً على ما كُلِّفْتَ به من أهْلٍ وَوَلَدٍ ونَفْسٍ ومَالٍ، وكُلِّ من تعلق بك من النساء والرجال.\nولمَّا وصف الحيُّ تعالى نفسه بأوصاف الكمال من الكبرياء والعظمة والجلال، وكانت شواهد ذلك ظاهرة في خلقه حتى تبيَّن الحق من الباطل، بيّن ذلك بقوله:","quran_ar":"اللَّهُ لاَ إِلهَ إِلاَّ هُوَ الْحَيُّ الْقَيُّومُ لا تَأْخُذُهُ سِنَةٌ وَلا نَوْمٌ لَهُ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ مَنْ ذَا الَّذِي يَشْفَعُ عِنْدَهُ إِلاَّ بِإِذْنِهِ يَعْلَمُ ما بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَما خَلْفَهُمْ وَلا يُحِيطُونَ بِشَيْءٍ مِنْ عِلْمِهِ إِلاَّ بِما شاءَ وَسِعَ كُرْسِيُّهُ السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضَ وَلا يَؤُدُهُ حِفْظُهُما وَهُوَ الْعَلِيُّ الْعَظِيمُ (255)","quran_en":"255. God—there is no god but He, the Living, the Self-Subsisting. Neither slumber nor sleep overtakes Him. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth. Who can intercede with Him except by His permission? He knows what is before them and what is behind them; they encompass nothing of His knowledge except what He wills. His Footstool embraces the heavens and the earth, and preserving them does not burden Him. He is the High, the Tremendous.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The divine Name and its predicates","html":"<p>I say: “God” is the subject, and the sentence “there is no god but He” its predicate. The separate pronoun “He” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هو<\/bdi>) substitutes for the pronoun implicit in the predicate. “The Living” is either a second predicate, the predicate of an omitted subject, or a substitute for “God.” <i>Qayyūm<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قيوم<\/bdi>) has the intensive pattern <i>fayʿūl<\/i>, from standing or subsisting (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القيام<\/bdi>). It means the One who subsists through Himself, independent of anything else.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “God” is the subject, and the sentence “there is no god but He” its predicate. The separate pronoun “He” ( هو ) substitutes for the pronoun implicit in the predicate. “The Living” is either a second predicate, the predicate of an omitted subject, or a substitute for “God.” Qayyūm ( قيوم ) has the intensive pattern fayʿūl , from standing or subsisting ( القيام ). It means the One who subsists through Himself, independent of anything else."},{"type":"theology","title":"Life, subsistence and transcendence","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: God, the Necessarily Existent, alone deserves worship. Whoever worships another has committed a tremendous wrong. “The Living”: enduring without beginning, abiding without cessation, beyond the reach of death and passing away. “The Self-Subsisting”: continually attending to the governance of His creation, conveying benefits, repelling harms, bringing provisions and the various forms of advancement.<\/p><p>“Neither slumber nor sleep overtakes Him.” Slumber (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السنة<\/bdi>) is the lassitude preceding sleep. Sleep is a condition that overtakes the human being when the nerves of the brain relax because of the moisture of ascending vapours, so that the outward senses cease altogether to perceive.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-218\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B201]<\/a><\/p><p>Slumber precedes sleep here in the order in which they occur, as in “They spend no small or great expenditure” [9:121]. Both are mentioned because negating slumber alone might suggest that sleep overcomes Him, since it is stronger; negating sleep alone might suggest that slumber affects Him, since it is lighter. The purpose is to declare Him transcendent above human afflictions and confirm that He is Living and Self-Subsisting. Someone overtaken by drowsiness or sleep has life affected by a deficiency and is limited in preservation and governance. Hence there is no coordinating conjunction here or before the subsequent sentences: each confirms the description “Living, Self-Subsisting.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: God, the Necessarily Existent, alone deserves worship. Whoever worships another has committed a tremendous wrong. “The Living”: enduring without beginning, abiding without cessation, beyond the reach of death and passing away. “The Self-Subsisting”: continually attending to the governance of His creation, conveying benefits, repelling harms, bringing provisions and the various forms of advancement. “Neither slumber nor sleep overtakes Him.” Slumber ( السنة ) is the lassitude preceding sleep. Sleep is a condition that overtakes the human being when the nerves of the brain relax because of the moisture of ascending vapours, so that the outward senses cease altogether to perceive. [B201] Slumber precedes sleep here in the order in which they occur, as in “They spend no small or great expenditure” [9:121]. Both are mentioned because negating slumber alone might suggest that sleep overcomes Him, since it is stronger; negating sleep alone might suggest that slumber affects Him, since it is lighter. The purpose is to declare Him transcendent above human afflictions and confirm that He is Living and Self-Subsisting. Someone overtaken by drowsiness or sleep has life affected by a deficiency and is limited in preservation and governance. Hence there is no coordinating conjunction here or before the subsequent sentences: each confirms the description “Living, Self-Subsisting.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The invocation of the Living, the Self-Subsisting","html":"<p>It has been transmitted that this is God’s Greatest Name. The Prophet said to Fāṭima: “What prevents you from listening to my counsel? Say morning and evening: ‘Living, Self-Subsisting One, through Your mercy I seek relief. Set all my affairs right for me, and do not entrust me to my self for the blink of an eye.’” Al-Nasāʾī related it.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-219\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B202]<\/a><\/p><p>Muslim transmits from Abū Mūsā: “The Messenger of God stood among us and spoke five sayings: ‘God, mighty and majestic, does not sleep, and it is not fitting that He sleep. He lowers the balance and raises it. The work of the night is raised to Him before the work of the day, and the work of the day before the work of the night. His veil is light’—in another transmission, ‘fire’—‘and were He to unveil it, the splendours of His Face would burn everything of His creation reached by His sight.’”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-220\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B203]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"It has been transmitted that this is God’s Greatest Name. The Prophet said to Fāṭima: “What prevents you from listening to my counsel? Say morning and evening: ‘Living, Self-Subsisting One, through Your mercy I seek relief. Set all my affairs right for me, and do not entrust me to my self for the blink of an eye.’” Al-Nasāʾī related it. [B202] Muslim transmits from Abū Mūsā: “The Messenger of God stood among us and spoke five sayings: ‘God, mighty and majestic, does not sleep, and it is not fitting that He sleep. He lowers the balance and raises it. The work of the night is raised to Him before the work of the day, and the work of the day before the work of the night. His veil is light’—in another transmission, ‘fire’—‘and were He to unveil it, the splendours of His Face would burn everything of His creation reached by His sight.’” [B203]"},{"type":"theology","title":"Sovereignty, intercession and knowledge","html":"<p>“To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth.” This confirms His sustaining governance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قيوميته<\/bdi>) and argues for His uniqueness in divinity. What is in them includes their constituent parts and the things distinct from those parts but situated within them, rational beings and others. It is therefore more comprehensive than “To Him belong the heavens, the earth and what is in them.” He owns all this without partner or rival. <i>Mā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ما<\/bdi>) is used because the non-rational is predominant.<\/p><p>“Who can intercede with Him except by His permission?” This makes clear the grandeur of His standing. No one approaches His rank so as to change what He wills through intercession and entreaty, much less resist Him through opposition or hostility. The question negates the possibility: no one intercedes for someone He intends to punish except by His leave. The associators claimed that the idols would intercede for them. He therefore declares that intercession with Him occurs only by His permission—meaning the intercession of the Prophet and of certain prophets, saints and angels.<\/p><p>“He knows what is before them and what is behind them”: what preceded and what follows them; or the reverse, since you face the future and leave the past behind. It is also said: what is before them of this world and behind them of the Hereafter; or conversely, since they advance and leave this world behind. Or He knows the good and evil they have sent before them and the deeds still to come—or the reverse. The meaning is that He encompasses all things, and nothing is hidden from Him.<\/p><p>“They encompass nothing of His knowledge except what He wills”: they encompass none of the things He knows except what He wills to disclose to them. This is coordinated with the preceding statement because the two together establish His unique possession of complete, intrinsic knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلم الذاتي التام<\/bdi>), indicating His oneness in Essence and attributes.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth.” This confirms His sustaining governance ( قيوميته ) and argues for His uniqueness in divinity. What is in them includes their constituent parts and the things distinct from those parts but situated within them, rational beings and others. It is therefore more comprehensive than “To Him belong the heavens, the earth and what is in them.” He owns all this without partner or rival. Mā ( ما ) is used because the non-rational is predominant. “Who can intercede with Him except by His permission?” This makes clear the grandeur of His standing. No one approaches His rank so as to change what He wills through intercession and entreaty, much less resist Him through opposition or hostility. The question negates the possibility: no one intercedes for someone He intends to punish except by His leave. The associators claimed that the idols would intercede for them. He therefore declares that intercession with Him occurs only by His permission—meaning the intercession of the Prophet and of certain prophets, saints and angels. “He knows what is before them and what is behind them”: what preceded and what follows them; or the reverse, since you face the future and leave the past behind. It is also said: what is before them of this world and behind them of the Hereafter; or conversely, since they advance and leave this world behind. Or He knows the good and evil they have sent before them and the deeds still to come—or the reverse. The meaning is that He encompasses all things, and nothing is hidden from Him. “They encompass nothing of His knowledge except what He wills”: they encompass none of the things He knows except what He wills to disclose to them. This is coordinated with the preceding statement because the two together establish His unique possession of complete, intrinsic knowledge ( العلم الذاتي التام ), indicating His oneness in Essence and attributes."},{"type":"language","title":"The Footstool and its interpretations","html":"<p>“His Footstool embraces the heavens and the earth.” One says that someone <i>yasaʿu<\/i> a thing when he can bear it, has power over it and can attend to it. One also says that a thing encompasses another when it surrounds and overwhelms it, so that the second becomes insignificant beside it. This is the appropriate meaning here.<\/p><p>The linguistic root of <i>kursī<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكرسي<\/bdi>) concerns the joining of one part to another. Hence <i>kurrāsa<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكراسة<\/bdi>), a gathering of written sheets, because its leaves are joined one upon another. In ordinary usage it denotes something upon which one sits, so named from the joining of its pieces of wood. There is disagreement about it: some identify it with the Throne, others distinguish them.<\/p><p>The correct view is that it is a tremendous created thing before the Throne, above the seven heavens and below the Throne. It is said that the heavens and earth beside the Footstool are like a ring in a desert, and the Footstool beside the Throne like a ring in a desert. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “The heavens within the Footstool are like seven dirhams upon a shield.” Another interpretation identifies His Footstool with His knowledge.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-221\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B204]<\/a><\/p><p>Al-Bayḍāwī said: it is a portrayal of His majesty and a purely figurative representation, like “They have not measured God with His true measure; the entire earth will be His handful on the Day of Resurrection, and the heavens folded in His right hand” [39:67]. There is in reality neither a seat nor someone seated. Alternatively, His Footstool is a metaphor for<\/p><p>His knowledge or sovereignty, drawn from the seat of learning or kingship. Or it is a body before the Throne, encompassing the seven heavens, in accordance with the Prophet’s saying: “The seven heavens and seven earths in the Footstool are only like a ring in a desert; the superiority of the Throne over the Footstool is like that desert’s superiority over that ring.” Perhaps it is the sphere known as the sphere of the zodiac. Here his words end. I say: al-Suyūṭī objected to this in his marginal commentary. God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-222\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B204]<\/a><\/p><p>“Preserving them does not burden Him”: preserving the heavens and earth does not weigh upon Him or cause Him difficulty. Their contents are not separately mentioned because preserving the two entails preserving what is in them. “He is the High”: exalted above likenesses and equals. “The Tremendous”: immense in standing and majestic in worth, beside whose greatness everything is deemed insignificant.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“His Footstool embraces the heavens and the earth.” One says that someone yasaʿu a thing when he can bear it, has power over it and can attend to it. One also says that a thing encompasses another when it surrounds and overwhelms it, so that the second becomes insignificant beside it. This is the appropriate meaning here. The linguistic root of kursī ( الكرسي ) concerns the joining of one part to another. Hence kurrāsa ( الكراسة ), a gathering of written sheets, because its leaves are joined one upon another. In ordinary usage it denotes something upon which one sits, so named from the joining of its pieces of wood. There is disagreement about it: some identify it with the Throne, others distinguish them. The correct view is that it is a tremendous created thing before the Throne, above the seven heavens and below the Throne. It is said that the heavens and earth beside the Footstool are like a ring in a desert, and the Footstool beside the Throne like a ring in a desert. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “The heavens within the Footstool are like seven dirhams upon a shield.” Another interpretation identifies His Footstool with His knowledge. [B204] Al-Bayḍāwī said: it is a portrayal of His majesty and a purely figurative representation, like “They have not measured God with His true measure; the entire earth will be His handful on the Day of Resurrection, and the heavens folded in His right hand” [39:67]. There is in reality neither a seat nor someone seated. Alternatively, His Footstool is a metaphor for His knowledge or sovereignty, drawn from the seat of learning or kingship. Or it is a body before the Throne, encompassing the seven heavens, in accordance with the Prophet’s saying: “The seven heavens and seven earths in the Footstool are only like a ring in a desert; the superiority of the Throne over the Footstool is like that desert’s superiority over that ring.” Perhaps it is the sphere known as the sphere of the zodiac. Here his words end. I say: al-Suyūṭī objected to this in his marginal commentary. God Most High knows best. [B204] “Preserving them does not burden Him”: preserving the heavens and earth does not weigh upon Him or cause Him difficulty. Their contents are not separately mentioned because preserving the two entails preserving what is in them. “He is the High”: exalted above likenesses and equals. “The Tremendous”: immense in standing and majestic in worth, beside whose greatness everything is deemed insignificant."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The principal divine questions gathered in the verse","html":"<p>This verse contains the principal questions concerning God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أمهات المسائل الإلهية<\/bdi>). It indicates that He exists and is one in divinity; possesses life intrinsically; is necessarily existent through Himself and brings others into existence, since the Self-Subsisting is the One who subsists through Himself and sustains another. He is transcendent above occupying a location and indwelling (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التحيز والحلول<\/bdi>), free from change and lassitude. He does not resemble bodily forms, nor is He subject to what affects spirits.<\/p><p>He owns the visible dominion and the unseen kingdom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الملك والملكوت<\/bdi>), originates roots and branches, and is possessed of mighty force. None intercedes with Him except one whom He permits. He knows all things, manifest and hidden, universal and particular. His sovereignty and power extend to everything that can be possessed and fall under power. No difficulty burdens Him; no affair distracts Him from another. He is exalted beyond the reach of imaginings, Tremendous beyond the comprehension of understandings.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"This verse contains the principal questions concerning God ( أمهات المسائل الإلهية ). It indicates that He exists and is one in divinity; possesses life intrinsically; is necessarily existent through Himself and brings others into existence, since the Self-Subsisting is the One who subsists through Himself and sustains another. He is transcendent above occupying a location and indwelling ( التحيز والحلول ), free from change and lassitude. He does not resemble bodily forms, nor is He subject to what affects spirits. He owns the visible dominion and the unseen kingdom ( الملك والملكوت ), originates roots and branches, and is possessed of mighty force. None intercedes with Him except one whom He permits. He knows all things, manifest and hidden, universal and particular. His sovereignty and power extend to everything that can be possessed and fall under power. No difficulty burdens Him; no affair distracts Him from another. He is exalted beyond the reach of imaginings, Tremendous beyond the comprehension of understandings."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The verse’s excellence","html":"<p>For this reason it is distinguished from its fellows by splendid virtues and exceptional properties. The Prophet said: “The greatest verse in the Qurʾān is the Verse of the Footstool.” He is also quoted as saying: “Whoever recites the Verse of the Footstool after each prescribed prayer is prevented from entering the Garden only by death”—and in another transmission: “The Possessor of Majesty and Generosity Himself undertakes the taking of his spirit.” Further: “Only one utterly truthful or devoted to worship persists in it. Whoever recites it upon taking to bed is made secure in himself, his neighbour, his neighbour’s neighbour and the houses around him.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-223\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B205]<\/a><\/p><p>He is also quoted as saying: “This verse is not recited in a house without the devils abandoning it for thirty days; no male or female sorcerer enters it for forty days. ʿAlī, teach it to your children, family and neighbours: no greater verse than it has been revealed.” Al-Bayḍāwī and Abū al-Suʿūd mention this. Al-Suyūṭī criticised some of these reports. Weak reports are acted upon concerning virtues. And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-224\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B205]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"For this reason it is distinguished from its fellows by splendid virtues and exceptional properties. The Prophet said: “The greatest verse in the Qurʾān is the Verse of the Footstool.” He is also quoted as saying: “Whoever recites the Verse of the Footstool after each prescribed prayer is prevented from entering the Garden only by death”—and in another transmission: “The Possessor of Majesty and Generosity Himself undertakes the taking of his spirit.” Further: “Only one utterly truthful or devoted to worship persists in it. Whoever recites it upon taking to bed is made secure in himself, his neighbour, his neighbour’s neighbour and the houses around him.” [B205] He is also quoted as saying: “This verse is not recited in a house without the devils abandoning it for thirty days; no male or female sorcerer enters it for forty days. ʿAlī, teach it to your children, family and neighbours: no greater verse than it has been revealed.” Al-Bayḍāwī and Abū al-Suʿūd mention this. Al-Suyūṭī criticised some of these reports. Weak reports are acted upon concerning virtues. And God Most High knows best. [B205]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Give knowledge, and meet God through God","html":"<p>You who believe with the faith of spiritual election: “Spend from what We have provided you” of abundant sciences and treasuries of understanding, before the day of meeting comes. On that day transactions fall away and those allusions disappear. Nothing avails except entering through the door of generosity,<\/p><p>so that one meets God through God, without anything else. Those who deny this wrong themselves: they rely upon their works and thus meet God with the greatest idol. The Living, the Self-Subsisting, the Great, the Exalted, is independent of benefiting from works. And success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"You who believe with the faith of spiritual election: “Spend from what We have provided you” of abundant sciences and treasuries of understanding, before the day of meeting comes. On that day transactions fall away and those allusions disappear. Nothing avails except entering through the door of generosity, so that one meets God through God, without anything else. Those who deny this wrong themselves: they rely upon their works and thus meet God with the greatest idol. The Living, the Self-Subsisting, the Great, the Exalted, is independent of benefiting from works. And success is through God."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Attachment and taking on the appropriate character","html":"<p>One who knows that He is the Living who does not die relies upon Him: “Rely upon the Living who does not die” [25:58]. Attachment to this Name (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التعلق<\/bdi>) is seeking the spirit’s life through knowledge and perfect love.<\/p><p>One who knows that He is the Living, the Self-Subsisting, trusts Him, forgets remembrance of everything through remembering Him, and witnesses no other through witnessing His sustaining governance. Attachment to this Name is seeking knowledge of His sustaining governance until one rests from the misery of self-directed planning. Taking on its appropriate character (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التخلق<\/bdi>) means attending to everything with which you have been charged: spouse, children, self, property, and all the women and men connected with you.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-225\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B206]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"One who knows that He is the Living who does not die relies upon Him: “Rely upon the Living who does not die” [25:58]. Attachment to this Name ( التعلق ) is seeking the spirit’s life through knowledge and perfect love. One who knows that He is the Living, the Self-Subsisting, trusts Him, forgets remembrance of everything through remembering Him, and witnesses no other through witnessing His sustaining governance. Attachment to this Name is seeking knowledge of His sustaining governance until one rests from the misery of self-directed planning. Taking on its appropriate character ( التخلق ) means attending to everything with which you have been charged: spouse, children, self, property, and all the women and men connected with you. [B206]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Living Most High has described Himself through the attributes of perfection—grandeur, might and majesty. Their evidences are manifest in His creation, so that truth is distinguished from falsehood. He makes this clear in the following words.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Living Most High has described Himself through the attributes of perfection—grandeur, might and majesty. Their evidences are manifest in His creation, so that truth is distinguished from falsehood. He makes this clear in the following words."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C285.1","page":285,"ar":"آف الطعام أوفا وآفة: فسد، والبلاد: أصابتها آفة من قحط أو مرض أو غيرهما.","en":"Āfa, with the verbal nouns awf and āfa: of food, it spoiled; of a country, it was struck by a calamity such as drought, disease or something else.","anchor":"مؤوف الحياة"},{"id":"C286.1","page":286,"ar":"هذا الذي اختاره جمع من الخلف، فرارا من توهم التجسيم، والحق: أن الكرسي ثابت كما نطقت به الأخبار الصحيحة. ومذهب سادتنا من السلف الصالح هو: جعل ذلك من الأمور التي لا يحيط المرء بها علما، مع تفويض العلم فيها إلى الله تعالى، مع اعتقاد التنزيه والتقديس لله تعالى عنها. وهذا هو الأسلم.","en":"This is the view chosen by a number of later scholars to avoid any suggestion of corporealism. The truth is that the Footstool is established, as sound transmitted reports state. The position of our masters among the righteous early generations is to treat this as one of the matters that a person cannot encompass in knowledge, entrusting knowledge of it to God Most High while believing God to be transcendent and holy above such things. This is the safer course.","anchor":"ولا كرسي في الحقيقة ولا قاعد"},{"id":"C287.1","page":287,"ar":"في حاشيته على البيضاوي، والمسماة نواهد الأبكار وشوارد الأفكار.","en":"In his supercommentary on al-Bayḍāwī, entitled Nawāhid al-abkār wa-shawārid al-afkār.","anchor":"في حاشيته عليه"}],"english_characters":10876,"arabic_characters":7292},{"id":"q2-256","q":"2:256","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:256 · No compulsion in religion and the firmest handhold","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 288–289","cairo":"I:288–289","source_page_first":288,"source_page_last":289,"ar":"لا إِكْراهَ فِي الدِّينِ قَدْ تَبَيَّنَ الرُّشْدُ مِنَ الْغَيِّ فَمَنْ يَكْفُرْ بِالطَّاغُوتِ وَيُؤْمِنْ بِاللَّهِ فَقَدِ اسْتَمْسَكَ بِالْعُرْوَةِ الْوُثْقى لا انْفِصامَ لَها وَاللَّهُ سَمِيعٌ عَلِيمٌ (256)\nقلت: (الرُشْد) : مصدر رَشُد، بالكسر والضم، رشداً ورشاداً، و (الغي) : مصدر غَوَى، إذا ضلّ فى مُعْتَقَدِه، و (الطاغوت) : فعلوت من الطغيان، وأصله: طغيوت، فقلبت لام الكلمة لعينها فصار طيغوت، ثم قلبت الياء ألفاً.\nوهو كل ما عُبد من دون الله راضياً بذلك، و (العروة) : ما تستمسك به اليد عند خوف الزل كالحبل ونحوه، ووثوقها: متانتها، وانفصامها أن تنفك عن موضعها، وأصل الفصم في اللغة: أن ينفك الخلخال ونحوه ولا يَبِين، فإذا بان فهو القَصْم- بالقاف- وهو هنا استعارة للدّين الصحيح.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في شأن رجلٍ من الأنصار، تَنَصَّر ولدَاه قبل البَعْثَة فلما جاء الإسلامُ قَدِمَا إلى المدينة فدعاهما أبوهما إلى الإسلام فامتنعا، فلزمهما أبوهما وقال: والله لا أدِعكما حتى تُسلما، فاختصموا إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فأنزل الله: لا إِكْراهَ فِي الدِّينِ، فهو خبر بمعنى النهي، أي: لا تُكرهوا أحداً على الدخول في الدين.\nوهو خاص بأهل الكتاب.\nقال البيضاوي: إذ الإكراه في الحقيقة هو: إلزام الغير فعلاً لا يرى فيه خيراً، ولكن قَدْ تَبَيَّنَ الرُّشْدُ مِنَ الْغَيِّ أي تميّز الإيمان من الكفر بالآيات الواضحة، ودلت الدلائل على أن الإيمان رشد يوصل إلى السعادة الأبدية، والكفر غيّ يوصل إلى الشقاوة السرمدية. والعاقل متى تبين له ذلك بادرت نفسُه إلى الإيمان طلباً للفوز بالسعادة والنجاة، ولم يحتج إلى الإكراه والإلجاء. هـ.\nفَمَنْ يَكْفُرْ بِالطَّاغُوتِ أي: يبعد عنها ويجحد ربوبيتها وَيُؤْمِنْ بِاللَّهِ أي: يصدق بوحدانيته، ويقر برسله، فَقَدِ اسْتَمْسَكَ بِالْعُرْوَةِ الْوُثْقى أي: فقد تمسك بالدين المتين، لا انقطاع له أبداً، وَاللَّهُ سَمِيعٌ بالأقوال، عَلِيمٌ بالنيات، فإنَّ الدين مشتمل على قول باللسان وعقد بالجَنَان، فحسن التعبير بصفة السمع والعلم.\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: قال في الحكم: «لا يخاف عليك أن تلتبس الطرق، إنما يخاف عليك من غلبة الهوى عليك» . وقال أحمد بن حضرويه: الطريق واضح، والحق لائح، والداعي قد أَسْمَع، ما التحير بعد هذا إلا من العمَى. هـ. فطريق السير واضحة لمَن سبقت له العناية، باقية إلى يوم القيامة، وكل ما سوى الله طاغوت، فمن أعرض عن السَّوَى، وعلق قلبه بمحبة المولى، فقد استمسك بالعروة الوثقى، التي لا انفصام لها على طول المدى. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم بيَّن الحق تعالى حال أهل العناية من أهل الشقاوة، فقال:","quran_ar":"لا إِكْراهَ فِي الدِّينِ قَدْ تَبَيَّنَ الرُّشْدُ مِنَ الْغَيِّ فَمَنْ يَكْفُرْ بِالطَّاغُوتِ وَيُؤْمِنْ بِاللَّهِ فَقَدِ اسْتَمْسَكَ بِالْعُرْوَةِ الْوُثْقى لا انْفِصامَ لَها وَاللَّهُ سَمِيعٌ عَلِيمٌ (256)","quran_en":"256. There is no compulsion in religion. Right guidance has become distinct from error. Whoever rejects false objects of worship and believes in God has grasped the firmest handhold, which will never break. God is Hearing, Knowing.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Guidance, error and the handhold","html":"<p>I say: <i>rushd<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرشد<\/bdi>) is the verbal noun of <i>rashida<\/i> or <i>rashuda<\/i>, alongside <i>rashād<\/i>. <i>Ghayy<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الغي<\/bdi>) is the verbal noun of <i>ghawā<\/i>: to go astray in belief. <i>Ṭāghūt<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطاغوت<\/bdi>) has the pattern <i>faʿalūt<\/i>, from transgression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطغيان<\/bdi>). Its original form is <i>ṭaghayūt<\/i>. The final root-letter is transposed into the middle position, giving <i>ṭayaghūt<\/i>, and the <i>yāʾ<\/i> then becomes <i>alif<\/i>. It denotes anything worshipped besides God while consenting to that worship.<\/p><p>The handhold (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العروة<\/bdi>) is what the hand grasps when one fears slipping, such as a rope. Its firmness is its strength; its breaking means becoming detached from its place. Originally <i>faṣm<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفصم<\/bdi>) means the cracking of an anklet or something similar without its parts separating. If they separate, it is <i>qaṣm<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القصم<\/bdi>), with <i>qāf<\/i>. Here the handhold is a metaphor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استعارة<\/bdi>) for sound religion.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: rushd ( الرشد ) is the verbal noun of rashida or rashuda , alongside rashād . Ghayy ( الغي ) is the verbal noun of ghawā : to go astray in belief. Ṭāghūt ( الطاغوت ) has the pattern faʿalūt , from transgression ( الطغيان ). Its original form is ṭaghayūt . The final root-letter is transposed into the middle position, giving ṭayaghūt , and the yāʾ then becomes alif . It denotes anything worshipped besides God while consenting to that worship. The handhold ( العروة ) is what the hand grasps when one fears slipping, such as a rope. Its firmness is its strength; its breaking means becoming detached from its place. Originally faṣm ( الفصم ) means the cracking of an anklet or something similar without its parts separating. If they separate, it is qaṣm ( القصم ), with qāf . Here the handhold is a metaphor ( استعارة ) for sound religion."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The distinction of guidance from error","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, speaks concerning a man of the Helpers whose two sons became Christians before the prophetic mission. When Islam came, they visited Medina. Their father invited them to Islam, but they refused. He held them and said: “By God, I shall not leave you until you become Muslims.” They brought their dispute to the Messenger of God, and God revealed: “There is no compulsion in religion.” It is a statement conveying a prohibition: do not compel anyone to enter religion. It applies specifically to the People of the Book.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-226\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B207]<\/a><\/p><p>Al-Bayḍāwī said: compulsion properly means obliging someone to perform an act in which he sees no good. But right guidance has become distinct from error: faith has been distinguished from unbelief through clear signs. The proofs establish that faith is guidance leading to everlasting happiness, while unbelief is error leading to perpetual wretchedness. Once this becomes clear to a rational person, his self hastens to faith, seeking happiness and salvation. He needs neither compulsion nor force. Here his words end.<\/p><p>Whoever rejects false objects of worship—distances himself from them and denies their Lordship—and believes in God, affirming His oneness and acknowledging His messengers, has grasped the firmest handhold: adhered to the strong religion that never breaks. God hears words and knows intentions. Since religion includes the tongue’s confession and the heart’s conviction, the attributes of hearing and knowledge are fittingly mentioned. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, speaks concerning a man of the Helpers whose two sons became Christians before the prophetic mission. When Islam came, they visited Medina. Their father invited them to Islam, but they refused. He held them and said: “By God, I shall not leave you until you become Muslims.” They brought their dispute to the Messenger of God, and God revealed: “There is no compulsion in religion.” It is a statement conveying a prohibition: do not compel anyone to enter religion. It applies specifically to the People of the Book. [B207] Al-Bayḍāwī said: compulsion properly means obliging someone to perform an act in which he sees no good. But right guidance has become distinct from error: faith has been distinguished from unbelief through clear signs. The proofs establish that faith is guidance leading to everlasting happiness, while unbelief is error leading to perpetual wretchedness. Once this becomes clear to a rational person, his self hastens to faith, seeking happiness and salvation. He needs neither compulsion nor force. Here his words end. Whoever rejects false objects of worship—distances himself from them and denies their Lordship—and believes in God, affirming His oneness and acknowledging His messengers, has grasped the firmest handhold: adhered to the strong religion that never breaks. God hears words and knows intentions. Since religion includes the tongue’s confession and the heart’s conviction, the attributes of hearing and knowledge are fittingly mentioned. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The clear path and the danger of desire","html":"<p>The <i>Ḥikam<\/i> says: “What is feared for you is not that the paths become confused, but that desire overcome you.” Aḥmad ibn Khaḍrawayh said: “The path is clear, the Real manifest, and the caller has made himself heard. Bewilderment after this comes only from blindness.”<\/p><p>The path of wayfaring is clear for someone preceded by divine care and remains until the Day of Resurrection.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-227\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B208]<\/a> Everything other than God is a false object of worship. Whoever turns away from otherness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السوى<\/bdi>) and attaches his heart to love of the Master has grasped the firmest handhold, which will not break throughout the ages. And success is through God; He guides to the level path.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Ḥikam says: “What is feared for you is not that the paths become confused, but that desire overcome you.” Aḥmad ibn Khaḍrawayh said: “The path is clear, the Real manifest, and the caller has made himself heard. Bewilderment after this comes only from blindness.” The path of wayfaring is clear for someone preceded by divine care and remains until the Day of Resurrection. [B208] Everything other than God is a false object of worship. Whoever turns away from otherness ( السوى ) and attaches his heart to love of the Master has grasped the firmest handhold, which will not break throughout the ages. And success is through God; He guides to the level path."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then distinguishes the condition of the people of divine care from that of the people of wretchedness.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then distinguishes the condition of the people of divine care from that of the people of wretchedness."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3163,"arabic_characters":2246},{"id":"q2-257","q":"2:257","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:257 · From darkness into light","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 289–290","cairo":"I:289–290","source_page_first":289,"source_page_last":290,"ar":"اللَّهُ وَلِيُّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا يُخْرِجُهُمْ مِنَ الظُّلُماتِ إِلَى النُّورِ وَالَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا أَوْلِياؤُهُمُ الطَّاغُوتُ يُخْرِجُونَهُمْ مِنَ النُّورِ إِلَى الظُّلُماتِ أُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (257)\nقلت: الولي: هو المحب الذي يتولى أمور محبوبه، أو الناصر الذي ينصر محبوبه، ولا يخذله بأن يكله إلى نفسه. وجملة (يخرجهم) : حال من الضمير المستتر في الخبر، أو من الموصول أو منهما، أو خبر ثان.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: اللَّهُ وَلِيُّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا أي: محبهم ومتولي أمورهم، يُخْرِجُهُمْ مِنَ ظلمات الكفر والجهل، ومتابعة الهوى وقبول الوسواس، والشبه المُشْكِلة في التوحيد- إلى نور الإيمان واليقين، وصحة التوحيد، ومتابعة الداعي إلى الله، وَالَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا أَوْلِياؤُهُمُ أي: أحباؤهم الطَّاغُوتُ أي: الشياطين، أو المضلات من الهوى والشيطان وغيرهما، يُخْرِجُونَهُمْ مِنَ النُّورِ الذي مُنحوه بالفطرة الأصلية، أو يصدونهم من الدخول في الإيمان إلى ظلمات الكفر والجهل، والتقليد الرديء واتباع الهوى، أُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ بسبب نِيَّاتهم البقاء على الكفر إلى الممات، ولم يذكر في جانب المؤمنين دخول الجنة لتكون عبادتهم عبودية، لا خوفاً ولا طمعاً. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: (الله ولي الذين آمنوا) حيث تولاهم بسابق العناية، وكلأهم بعين الرعاية، يخرجهم أولا من ظلمات الكفر إلى نور الإيمان، ثم من ظلمات الحس ورؤية الأكوان إلى نور المعاني بحصول الشهود والعيان، فافن\nعن الإحساس تر عبراً. «الكون كله ظلمة، وإنما أناره ظهورُ الحَقِّ فيه» . أو تقول: الكون كله ظلمة لأهل الحجاب، وأما عند أهل المعرفة فالكون عندهم كله نور، وإنما حجبه ظهور الحكمة فيه، «فمن رأى الكون ولم يشهد النور فيه، أو قبله، أو بعده، فقد أعوزه وجود الأنوار وحجبت عنه شموس المعارف بسحب الآثار» . والذين كفروا- وهم الذين سبق لهم الشقاء، وحكم عليهم بالبعد القدر والقضاء- أولياؤهم الطاغوت، وهم القواطع: من الهوى والشيطان والدنيا والناس، (يخرجونهم من النور إلى الظلمات) أي: يمنعونهم من شهود تلك الأنوار السابقة، إلى الوقوف مع تلك الظلمات المتقدمة، فهم متعاكسون مع مَن سبقتْ لهم العناية، فما خرج منه أهل العناية وقع فيه أهل الغواية. نسأل الله الحفظ والعافية فى الدنيا والآخرة.\nثم بيَّن الحق تعالى حال مَن سبق له الشقاء، فقال:","quran_ar":"اللَّهُ وَلِيُّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا يُخْرِجُهُمْ مِنَ الظُّلُماتِ إِلَى النُّورِ وَالَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا أَوْلِياؤُهُمُ الطَّاغُوتُ يُخْرِجُونَهُمْ مِنَ النُّورِ إِلَى الظُّلُماتِ أُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (257)","quran_en":"257. God is the protecting friend of those who believe: He brings them out of darkness into light. As for those who disbelieve, their protecting friends are false objects of worship, bringing them out of light into darkness. They are the people of the Fire, abiding therein.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The protecting friend","html":"<p>I say: a <i>walī<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولي<\/bdi>) is the lover who takes charge of his beloved’s affairs, or the helper who assists his beloved and does not abandon him by entrusting him to himself. The sentence “He brings them out” is a circumstance referring to the pronoun implicit in the predicate, to the relative pronoun, or to both; alternatively, it is a second predicate.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: a walī ( الولي ) is the lover who takes charge of his beloved’s affairs, or the helper who assists his beloved and does not abandon him by entrusting him to himself. The sentence “He brings them out” is a circumstance referring to the pronoun implicit in the predicate, to the relative pronoun, or to both; alternatively, it is a second predicate."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Faith, original disposition and corrupt guidance","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: God is the protecting friend of those who believe—their lover and the One who takes charge of their affairs. He brings them out of the darkness of unbelief, ignorance, following desire, accepting whisperings and troubling doubts about unity, into the light of faith, certainty, sound affirmation of unity and following the caller to God.<\/p><p>As for those who disbelieve, their protecting friends—their beloved allies—are the false objects of worship: devils, or the misleading forces of desire, Satan and others. They bring them out of the light granted them through their original disposition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفطرة الأصلية<\/bdi>), or prevent them from entering faith, into the darkness of unbelief, ignorance, corrupt imitation and following desire.<\/p><p>They are the people of the Fire, abiding therein, because they intend to remain in unbelief until death. On the believers’ side, entrance into the Garden is not mentioned, so that their worship may be servanthood, not fear or desire for gain. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: God is the protecting friend of those who believe—their lover and the One who takes charge of their affairs. He brings them out of the darkness of unbelief, ignorance, following desire, accepting whisperings and troubling doubts about unity, into the light of faith, certainty, sound affirmation of unity and following the caller to God. As for those who disbelieve, their protecting friends—their beloved allies—are the false objects of worship: devils, or the misleading forces of desire, Satan and others. They bring them out of the light granted them through their original disposition ( الفطرة الأصلية ), or prevent them from entering faith, into the darkness of unbelief, ignorance, corrupt imitation and following desire. They are the people of the Fire, abiding therein, because they intend to remain in unbelief until death. On the believers’ side, entrance into the Garden is not mentioned, so that their worship may be servanthood, not fear or desire for gain. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The world as darkness and as light","html":"<p>“God is the protecting friend of those who believe”: He takes charge of them through prior divine care and guards them with the eye of solicitude. First He brings them from the darkness of unbelief into the light of faith; then from the darkness of sensation and seeing created things into the light of meanings, through the attainment of witnessing and direct vision. Become effaced<\/p><p>from sensation, and you will see lessons.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-228\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B209]<\/a><\/p><p>“The whole cosmos is darkness; only the manifestation of the Real within it illuminates it.” Or you may say: the whole cosmos is darkness for the veiled, whereas for the people of gnosis it is all light; the manifestation of wisdom within it is what veils it. “Whoever sees the cosmos without witnessing the light within it, before it or after it lacks the presence of lights; the suns of gnoses are veiled from him by the clouds of created effects.”<\/p><p>Those who disbelieve—those preceded by wretchedness and sentenced to distance by destiny and decree—have false objects of worship as their protecting friends: the highwaymen of desire, Satan, the world and people. “They bring them out of light into darkness”: they prevent them from witnessing the lights just mentioned and hold them at the darkness described before. Their direction is the opposite of those preceded by divine care. What the people of divine care leave, the people of error fall into. We ask God for preservation and well-being in this world and the Hereafter.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"“God is the protecting friend of those who believe”: He takes charge of them through prior divine care and guards them with the eye of solicitude. First He brings them from the darkness of unbelief into the light of faith; then from the darkness of sensation and seeing created things into the light of meanings, through the attainment of witnessing and direct vision. Become effaced from sensation, and you will see lessons. [B209] “The whole cosmos is darkness; only the manifestation of the Real within it illuminates it.” Or you may say: the whole cosmos is darkness for the veiled, whereas for the people of gnosis it is all light; the manifestation of wisdom within it is what veils it. “Whoever sees the cosmos without witnessing the light within it, before it or after it lacks the presence of lights; the suns of gnoses are veiled from him by the clouds of created effects.” Those who disbelieve—those preceded by wretchedness and sentenced to distance by destiny and decree—have false objects of worship as their protecting friends: the highwaymen of desire, Satan, the world and people. “They bring them out of light into darkness”: they prevent them from witnessing the lights just mentioned and hold them at the darkness described before. Their direction is the opposite of those preceded by divine care. What the people of divine care leave, the people of error fall into. We ask God for preservation and well-being in this world and the Hereafter."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then illustrates the condition of someone preceded by wretchedness.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then illustrates the condition of someone preceded by wretchedness."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2934,"arabic_characters":2017},{"id":"q2-258","q":"2:258","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:258 · Abraham’s argument and the false claim to Lordship","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 290–291","cairo":"I:290–291","source_page_first":290,"source_page_last":291,"ar":"أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِي حَاجَّ إِبْراهِيمَ فِي رَبِّهِ أَنْ آتاهُ اللَّهُ الْمُلْكَ إِذْ قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ رَبِّيَ الَّذِي يُحْيِي وَيُمِيتُ قالَ أَنَا أُحْيِي وَأُمِيتُ قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يَأْتِي بِالشَّمْسِ مِنَ الْمَشْرِقِ فَأْتِ بِها مِنَ الْمَغْرِبِ فَبُهِتَ الَّذِي كَفَرَ وَاللَّهُ لا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الظَّالِمِينَ (258)\nقلت: (أن آتاه) : على حذف لام العلة، و (إذ قال) : ظرف ل- (حاجّ) ، أو بدل من (آتاه الله) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله متعجباً من جهالة النمرود، والمراد تعجيب السامع: أَلَمْ تَرَ يا محمد، إِلَى جهالة الَّذِي حَاجَّ إِبْراهِيمَ أي: خاصمه فِي رَبِّهِ لأجْل أَنْ أعطاه اللَّهُ الْمُلْكَ، أي: حمله على ذلك بطر الملك. وذلك أنه لما كسَّر إبراهيم الأصنام، سجنه أياماً، وأخرجه من السجن، وقال له: من ربك الذي تعبد؟ قالَ له إِبْراهِيمَ عليه السلام: رَبِّيَ الَّذِي يُحْيِي وَيُمِيتُ، أي: يخلق الأرواح في الأجسام، ويخرجها عند انقضاء آجالها، (قال) نمرود: أَنَا أُحْيِي وَأُمِيتُ، فدعا برجلين فقتل أحدهما، وعفا عن الآخر، فلما رأى إبراهيم عليه السلام غلطه وتشغيبه عدل له إلى حجة أخرى، لا مقدور للبشر على الإتيان بمثلها، فقال له: فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يَأْتِي بِالشَّمْسِ مِنَ الْمَشْرِقِ فَأْتِ بِها أنت مِنَ الْمَغْرِبِ لأنك تدَّعي الربوبية، ومن شأن الربوبية أن تقدر على كل شيء، ولا يعجزها شيء، فَبُهِتَ الَّذِي كَفَرَ أي: غُلب وصار مبهوتاً، وَاللَّهُ لا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الظَّالِمِينَ إلى قبول الهداية، أو إلى طريق النجاة، أو إلى محجة الاحتجاج.\nالإشارة: قال بعض الحكماء: للنفس سر، ظهر على فرعون والنمرود، حتى صرّحا بدعوى الربوبية. قلت:\nوهذا السر هو ثابت للروح في أصل نشأتها لأنها جاءت من عالم العز والكبرياء. انظر قوله تعالى: وَنَفَخْتُ فِيهِ مِنْ رُوحِي، وقال أيضاً: قُلِ الرُّوحُ مِنْ أَمْرِ رَبِّي أي: سر من أسراره، فلما ركبت في هذا القالب الذي هو قالب العبودية- طلبت الرجوع إلى أصلها. فجعل لها الحقّ جلّ جلاله باباً تدخل منه فترجع إلى أصلها وهو الذل والخضوع والانكسار والافتقار، فمن دخل من هذا الباب، واتصل بمن يعرّفه ربه، رجعت روحه إلى ذلك الأصل، وأدركت ذلك السر، فمنها من تتسع لذلك السر وتطيقه، ومنها من تضيق عن حمله وتبوح به، فتقتلها الشريعة، كالحلاج وأمثاله، ومن طلب الرجوع إلى ذلك الأصل من غير بابه، ورام إدراكه بالعز والتكبّر، طُرد وأُبعد، وهو الذي صدر من النمرود وفرعون وغيرهما ممن ادّعى الربوبية جهلاً. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى من أدركته العناية، وفي قصته برهان على إحياء الموتى الذي احتج به إبراهيم- عليه السّلام- فقال:","quran_ar":"أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِي حَاجَّ إِبْراهِيمَ فِي رَبِّهِ أَنْ آتاهُ اللَّهُ الْمُلْكَ إِذْ قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ رَبِّيَ الَّذِي يُحْيِي وَيُمِيتُ قالَ أَنَا أُحْيِي وَأُمِيتُ قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يَأْتِي بِالشَّمْسِ مِنَ الْمَشْرِقِ فَأْتِ بِها مِنَ الْمَغْرِبِ فَبُهِتَ الَّذِي كَفَرَ وَاللَّهُ لا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الظَّالِمِينَ (258)","quran_en":"258. Have you not considered the man who disputed with Abraham concerning his Lord because God had given him kingship? Abraham said, “My Lord is He who gives life and causes death.” He said, “I give life and cause death.” Abraham said, “God brings the sun from the east; then bring it from the west.” The unbeliever was confounded. God does not guide the wrongdoing people.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The cause and the time of the dispute","html":"<p>I say: “Because He gave him” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أن آتاه<\/bdi>) has an omitted causal <i>lām<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لام العلة<\/bdi>). “When Abraham said” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إذ قال<\/bdi>) is an adverbial expression attached to “disputed,” or a substitute for “God gave him.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Because He gave him” ( أن آتاه ) has an omitted causal lām ( لام العلة ). “When Abraham said” ( إذ قال ) is an adverbial expression attached to “disputed,” or a substitute for “God gave him.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Power over subjects is not power to create life","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, expresses wonder at Nimrod’s ignorance, intending to awaken the listener’s wonder: have you not considered, Muḥammad, the ignorance of the man who disputed with Abraham concerning his Lord because God had given him kingship? The arrogance engendered by kingship drove him to it.<\/p><p>When Abraham broke the idols, Nimrod imprisoned him for some days, then brought him out and asked: “Who is your Lord whom you worship?” Abraham replied: “My Lord is He who gives life and causes death”—creates spirits in bodies and brings them out when their appointed terms expire. Nimrod said: “I give life and cause death.” He summoned two men, killed one and pardoned the other.<\/p><p>When Abraham saw his error and sophistry, he turned to another proof whose like no human could produce: “God brings the sun from the east; then bring it from the west.” You claim Lordship, and Lordship must have power over everything, with nothing beyond it. The unbeliever was confounded: defeated and bewildered. God does not guide the wrongdoing people to accept guidance, to the path of salvation or to the clear way of argument.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, expresses wonder at Nimrod’s ignorance, intending to awaken the listener’s wonder: have you not considered, Muḥammad, the ignorance of the man who disputed with Abraham concerning his Lord because God had given him kingship? The arrogance engendered by kingship drove him to it. When Abraham broke the idols, Nimrod imprisoned him for some days, then brought him out and asked: “Who is your Lord whom you worship?” Abraham replied: “My Lord is He who gives life and causes death”—creates spirits in bodies and brings them out when their appointed terms expire. Nimrod said: “I give life and cause death.” He summoned two men, killed one and pardoned the other. When Abraham saw his error and sophistry, he turned to another proof whose like no human could produce: “God brings the sun from the east; then bring it from the west.” You claim Lordship, and Lordship must have power over everything, with nothing beyond it. The unbeliever was confounded: defeated and bewildered. God does not guide the wrongdoing people to accept guidance, to the path of salvation or to the clear way of argument."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Return through humility, not through arrogance","html":"<p>One of the sages said: the self contains a mystery which appeared in Pharaoh and Nimrod until they openly claimed Lordship. I say: this mystery belongs to the spirit in its original arising, because it came from the world of might and grandeur. Consider: “I breathed into him of My spirit” [15:29]; and, “Say: the spirit is of my Lord’s command” [17:85]—a mystery among His mysteries.<\/p><p>When it was placed in this bodily mould, the mould of servanthood, it sought to return to its origin. The Real, glorious is His majesty, made a door through which it could enter and return: lowliness, submission, brokenness and need. Whoever enters by this door and joins someone who makes his Lord known to him has his spirit returned to that origin and apprehends that mystery. Some spirits have room for it and can bear it; others lack the capacity to bear it and divulge it, whereupon the Sacred Law puts them to death—as with al-Ḥallāj and those like him.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-229\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B210]<\/a><\/p><p>Whoever seeks to return to that origin by another door and attempts to apprehend it through might and arrogance is expelled and banished. This is what occurred with Nimrod, Pharaoh and others who ignorantly claimed Lordship. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"One of the sages said: the self contains a mystery which appeared in Pharaoh and Nimrod until they openly claimed Lordship. I say: this mystery belongs to the spirit in its original arising, because it came from the world of might and grandeur. Consider: “I breathed into him of My spirit” [15:29]; and, “Say: the spirit is of my Lord’s command” [17:85]—a mystery among His mysteries. When it was placed in this bodily mould, the mould of servanthood, it sought to return to its origin. The Real, glorious is His majesty, made a door through which it could enter and return: lowliness, submission, brokenness and need. Whoever enters by this door and joins someone who makes his Lord known to him has his spirit returned to that origin and apprehends that mystery. Some spirits have room for it and can bear it; others lack the capacity to bear it and divulge it, whereupon the Sacred Law puts them to death—as with al-Ḥallāj and those like him. [B210] Whoever seeks to return to that origin by another door and attempts to apprehend it through might and arrogance is expelled and banished. This is what occurred with Nimrod, Pharaoh and others who ignorantly claimed Lordship. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then mentions someone reached by divine care. His story also supplies a demonstration of the raising of the dead with which Abraham argued.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then mentions someone reached by divine care. His story also supplies a demonstration of the raising of the dead with which Abraham argued."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2683,"arabic_characters":2261}];
